// ManAlert — alert (generated from docs/shell/man-alert.md).
const ManAlert = "ALERT(1)                        Weft Commands                         ALERT(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  alert — evaluate conditions and dispatch notifications\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    <pipeline> | alert [<condition>] [--notify=<alias>]\n                       [-v <field>] [-t|--tag <field>]\n                       [--duration <dur>] [--average] [--msg <text>]\n\n    <pipeline> | alert (--if-zero <v> | --if-nonzero <v>) [--notify=<alias>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  alert reads a value from stdin and evaluates it against a condition. If the\n  condition holds, it writes the alert message to stderr and, when --notify is\n  supplied, dispatches the alias from /etc/notify.yaml to its Slack, PagerDuty,\n  webhook, and/or email targets (see Notifications (help alerts)). Its value\n  and tag flags can serve as a shorthand for shaping what gets persisted in a\n  cron (man crons).\n\n  With no condition the default is any: the alert fires whenever stdin is\n  non-empty. alert alone is a dry run that writes to stderr.\n\n  alert must be the last stage of its pipeline, and the last (or only) command\n  of a script.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCONDITIONS\x1b[0m\n  - any — fires whenever stdin is non-empty (the default when no condition is given)\n  - above <N> — current value is greater than N\n  - below <N> — current value is less than N\n  - eq <N> — current value equals N\n  - neq <N> — current value does not equal N\n  - changed — value differs from previous run\n  - absent — no input received in the window\n  - rising <N>% — value increased by N% compared to previous/window average\n  - falling <N>% — value decreased by N% compared to previous/window average\n  - matches <regex> — value matches a regular expression (RE2)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOPTIONS\x1b[0m\n  - -v <field>, --value <field> — column or JSON path holding the number to\n    compare on each line. If unspecified, the whole input must parse as a float,\n    unless the condition is any, absent and changed.\n  - -t <field>, --tag <field> — column or JSON path by which alerts should be\n    grouped. Combine with -v when you want to alert separately by host, for\n    example.\n\n  In a cron, -v and -t obviate the need for the # value:/# tag:\n  headers (man crons) a cron without alert would use. A cron that\n  alerts on tagged lines or JSON must give -v; without it, output will fail\n  runtime type checks (man crons) and automatically be disabled.\n  If the stdin to alert is typed as text (man crons), the condition\n  must be one of any, absent, changed and matches.\n\n  - --duration <dur> — time window for sustained conditions. For\n    above/below, condition must hold for all runs in the window. For\n    rising/falling, compares against the window average. For absent, no\n    non-empty output in the window. Units are s, m and h only.\n  - --average — for above/below with --duration, compare the rolling\n    average over the window against the threshold. No effect without\n    --duration.\n  - --msg <text> — override the default alert message.\n  - --notify=<alias> — dispatch to the named alias in /etc/notify.yaml (see\n    Notifications (help alerts)). Without --notify, alert writes to stderr.\n  - --if-zero <status> / --if-nonzero <status> — fire based on a shell exit\n    status instead of a stdin value. Pass the status of the command just before\n    alert as \"$?\". --if-zero fires when the status is 0, --if-nonzero is\n    the inverse. Mutually exclusive with the condition keywords. Useful in longer\n    scripts.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    # Alert if the range holds any ERROR line.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 0 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert when the range holds more than 50 errors.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=critical\n\n    # check_errors is a script in /bin that prints a numeric\n    check_errors | alert above 50 --duration 5m --average --notify=ops\n    check_errors | alert rising 30% --notify=critical\n\n    # Alert when the range holds no canary line at all.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/canary/main.log | wc -l | alert eq 0 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert when a lease changes hands: a text cron, where lease-holders is a\n    # /bin script printing \"<holder> <lease>\" lines.\n    #   # schedule: */2 * * * *\n    #   # type: text\n    lease-holders | alert changed -v 1 -t 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # Page when any host boots a release candidate; the cron still stores every\n    # host's build.\n    #   # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    #   # type: text\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep 'starting version' \\\n        | awk '{v[HOST]=$NF} END {for (h in v) print v[h], h}' \\\n        | alert matches '-rc[0-9]*$' -v 1 -t 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # One page if any host's disk use is above 80%.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n        | awk '{print HOST, $5}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert per host whose disk use is above 80%.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n        | awk '{print HOST, $5}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # Same idea with JSON rows\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLayering crons for richer alerts\x1b[0m\n  A cron run, and thereby an alert reads only the logs from its own schedule\n  interval: a */5 cron sees the last five minutes. However, you can leverage\n  the stored results of other crons using the weft::* predicates.\n\n  The simplest layer reads another cron's history with an aggregate and pipes\n  the number to alert:\n\n    # /cron.d/error-rate-hourly\n    # schedule: every 5m\n    weft::avg error-rate 1h | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  If you want to use boolean logic to add multiple such predicates, just add one\n  more condition and echo if nothing's failed yet:\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo 1) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  If your love of bash is less than it should be, you may want to stuff a Lua\n  script in /bin and pipe that to alert.\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    local rate = 0\n    if not weft.is_absent(\"error-rate\") then\n      rate = tonumber(weft.latest(\"error-rate\")) or 0\n    end\n    local canary_dead = weft.is_absent(\"canary-ping\")\n    local in_maintenance = not weft.is_absent(\"maintenance-flag\")\n\n    if rate > 50 and canary_dead and not in_maintenance then\n      io.stderr:write(string.format(\"composite fire: rate=%.1f canary_dead=%s\\n\",\n        rate, tostring(canary_dead)))\n      print(1)\n    else\n      print(0)\n    end\n\n  We'll write that script to /bin/deploy_danger, and then we can write this for\n  our alerting cron:\n\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    deploy_danger | alert above 0 --notify=critical\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, help alerts, help cron, man alerts (alerting strategies and notifications), man crons, man range, vi, cat\n"

// ManChart — chart (generated from docs/shell/man-chart.md).
const ManChart = "CHART(1)                        Weft Commands                         CHART(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  chart — render a chart in the terminal, from a pipeline or a cron\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    chart <cron>       [-a <fn>] [--bucket <dur>] [--kind KIND] [--title <text>]\n                       [-f|--follow [-i INTERVAL]]\n    <pipeline> | chart [-x <field>] [-y <field>] [-a <fn>] [-t <field>]\n                       [--bucket <dur>] [--kind KIND] [--title <text>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  chart has two forms:\n\n  Standalone: chart <cron> draws what a cron has\n  stored (man crons) over the current range. A tagged cron\n  draws one line per tag for the top eight series, with the rest collapsed into a\n  single line.  With --follow, the chart redraws every INTERVAL until you\n  exit. This requires the current range to be relative.\n\n  End of pipeline: <pipeline> | chart [...] draws the pipeline's output\n  once and returns to the prompt. chart must be the last stage; piping its\n  output onward is an error. By default, the x axis unit is line number of the\n  input, and the y axis values are the first whitespace-separated field of each\n  line. Both can be overridden with flags.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOPTIONS\x1b[0m\n  - -x <field>[::time[(<format>)]]: <field> is either a 1-indexed whitespace\n    column (-x 1) or a .-prefixed JSON path (-x .ts). Derived automatically\n    for crons. Value parse as an integer unless cast:\n      - ::time: x is a time in RFC3339 or epoch seconds (-x 1::time).\n      - ::time(<format>): x is a time in a strftime format A row that doesn't\n        match the supplied format aborts the chart with an error.\n      - You can supply a column range <start>-<end> when casting to time for\n        stamps that span two fields: -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)'.\n\n  - -y <field>:  same grammar as -x, except does not support time casts. Derived\n    automatically for crons\n  - -a, --aggregate <fn>: combines y values with the same x value with\n    count, sum, avg, min, max, p50, p95, identity.  Defaults to\n    identity, or count when --bucket is set.\n  - -t, --tag <field>: draw one line per distinct value of <field>, such\n    as one per host. Same field grammar as -x and -y. Only valid with --kind\n    line (the default when --tag is set). Each line gets its own color and a\n    legend row.\n  - --bucket <DURATION>: group rows into time buckets on x. Arguments formatted\n    as \\d[.\\d](../manual/ms|s|m|h).\n  - --kind <line|sparkline|bar>: chart kind. Default bar, or line when\n    --bucket or --tag is set.\n  - --title <text>: chart title.\n\n  -x, -y and -t must all be columns or all be JSON paths. -t\n  needs one JSON object per line, so use jq -c.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    # One bar per line\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq '.memory.pct' | chart\n\n    # Error lines per five minutes. The stamp \"2026/10/13 15:04:05\" spans\n    # columns 1 and 2.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR \\\n      | chart -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)' --bucket 5m\n\n    # 95th-percentile memory use per minute from JSON fields\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --bucket 1m -a p95\n\n    # One bar per host, average memory use\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .host -y .pct -a avg\n\n    # One line per host over time.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --tag .host --bucket 1m -a avg\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help cron, man dashboard, man range, and Dashboards (man dashboards) for a walkthrough from one-off charts to dashboards\n"

// ManClipboard — clipboard (generated from docs/shell/man-clipboard.md).
const ManClipboard = "CLIPBOARD(1)                    Weft Commands                     CLIPBOARD(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  clipboard — copy a short, shareable link to a /bin script\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    clipboard /bin/<name>\n    clipboard /bin/<name> --range=<spec>\n    clipboard /bin/<name> -- <script-args>...\n    clipboard /bin/<name> --range=<spec> -- <script-args>...\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  clipboard records the current shell's view of a /bin/ script and\n  pushes a short link to it (https://w3ft.net/s/k3Fz9aQ2xB; the host depends on your deployment) onto the\n  system clipboard. A teammate who opens the link — signed in to the\n  same org — lands in the web shell with the script invocation\n  pre-filled, ready to run with a single Enter. The script must already\n  exist under /bin. Anyone outside the org\n  gets \"that link doesn't exist\".\n\n  Weft stores, behind the short name:\n\n  - the script path (/bin/<name>),\n  - the time range used for log queries,\n  - any positional or flag args you typed after --.\n\n  Sharing the same script, range and args again gives the same link.\n\n  The recipient sees the script invocation in their prompt, can edit\n  it, and presses Enter to run it. Scripts resolve to the latest\n  version when the recipient runs them.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRANGE\x1b[0m\n  Without --range, clipboard captures the shell's current range\n  (the window range shows). To override, pass --range=<spec> using\n  the same syntax as the range command — 2h.., 1d..,\n  2026-04-12..2026-04-13, etc. See man range.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSCRIPT ARGS\x1b[0m\n  Everything after a -- separator becomes the script's arguments,\n  joined with single spaces. Other whitespace and shell quoting from\n  the original invocation are not preserved.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mERRORS\x1b[0m\n  clipboard fails with no such script /bin/<name> if the script does\n  not exist, first argument must be a /bin/<name> path for any other\n  first argument, and invalid range for a bad --range.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCLIPBOARD MECHANISM\x1b[0m\n  The command emits an OSC-52 escape sequence containing the link.\n  Modern terminal emulators (iTerm2, kitty, WezTerm, xterm with\n  OSC52 enabled, the weft web shell) intercept it and write to the\n  system clipboard. Terminals without OSC-52 support ignore the\n  sequence. The link is also printed to the screen so you can select\n  and copy it manually.\n\n  Over SSH, OSC-52 works: the escape sequence travels with the\n  rest of the terminal output and the local terminal performs the\n  clipboard write.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate\n                            link with current range, no args\n\n    clipboard /bin/error-rate -- --app=loom --since=1h\n                            link that pre-fills error-rate --app=loom --since=1h\n\n    clipboard /bin/scan --range=24h..\n                            link that pins a 24-hour window regardless of\n                            the sharer's current range\n\n    clipboard /bin/check --range=yesterday..today -- prod\n                            link to /bin/check with one positional arg\n                            and yesterday's window\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNOT SUPPORTED\x1b[0m\n  - Anything but a /bin/<name> script: crons, dashboards and unsaved\n    pipelines can't be shared this way. Save the pipeline as a script first.\n  - Reading from a pipe. clipboard ignores standard input.\n  - Pinning a link to a past version of the script (--version=): a link\n    always opens the script's current version.\n  - --range <spec> with a space. Write --range=<spec>.\n\n  Other options are rejected with clipboard: unknown option <flag>.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  range, git\n"

// ManDashboard — dashboard (generated from docs/shell/man-dashboard.md).
const ManDashboard = "DASHBOARD(1)                    Weft Commands                     DASHBOARD(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  dashboard — view a named tile layout in the terminal\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    dashboard\n    dashboard <name>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  Dashboards are YAML files under /etc/dashboards/. Create one with\n  vi /etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml; writing under /etc needs the\n  configmgr group.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mYAML GRAMMAR\x1b[0m\n  The dashboard YAML schema has lets you have one flat bucket for your tiles. You can also group your tiles into sections. The top-level keys in the schema are as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField     Required  Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------  --------  ----------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  title     no        Heading at the top of the dashboard\n  sections  one of    Array of sections, each with an optional title and a\n                      required tiles list\n  tiles     one of    A flat tile list (one untitled section)\n\n  A simple example dashboard with sections:\n\n    title: Production\n    sections:\n      - title: Errors\n        tiles:\n          - kind: sparkline\n            cron: error-rate\n            size: md\n      - title: Capacity\n        tiles:\n          - kind: counter\n            cron: pending-jobs\n            format: \"%d jobs\"\n            size: sm\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTile kinds\x1b[0m\n  Dashboard tiles are built on the same foundation as the chart command, so you can easily convert a 1-liner to to the appropriate YAML. For example,chart error-rate --kind bar -a max --bucket 1h --title \"Worst hour\" becomes:\n\n    - kind: bar\n      cron: error-rate\n      aggregate: max\n      bucket: 1h\n      title: Worst hour\n\n  In addition to the chart kinds (line, sparkline, and bar), dashboards\n  provide a few more options for laying out values on a tile grid where, in the\n  terminal, normal output works just fine:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKind     Default display                    Extra options\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ---------------------------------  ----------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  counter  latest value (on a tagged cron,    format -- strftime e.g. \"%d ms\",\n           the highest)                       `\"%.1f%%\"\n  gauge    latest value as a bar from 0 to    max\n           max (default 100)\n  table    latest run, row per tag            -\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRendering alerts\x1b[0m\n  If a tile targets a cron that uses alert, weft  will render that alert level\n  automatically as a part of the tile wherever possible:\n\n  - Lines, sparklines, and bar charts draw the cutoff as a red line\n  - Counters and gauges shows their content in red when the value exceeds the threshold\n  - Tables highlight offending rows in red\n\n  A counter, gauge or table that aggregates over a range compares the aggregate,\n  not the latest run. Other alert conditions, such as rising or absent, have\n  no single number to draw. To change where a tile turns red, change the alert.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mLAYOUT\x1b[0m\n  Tiles appear in YAML order, flowing left to right and then down. You can\n  specify width of a given tile with the optional size key. Valid values are\n  sm, md, and lg. lg will always be full width, with packing of sm and\n  md tiles best effort in both browser and terminal.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    dashboard                   list dashboards\n    dashboard ops               open the \"ops\" dashboard (create it first)\n    vi /etc/dashboards/ops.yaml create or edit it\n    rm /etc/dashboards/ops.yaml delete it (its crons keep running)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help cron, man chart, and Dashboards (man dashboards) for a walkthrough from one-off charts to dashboards\n"

// ManExplain — explain (generated from docs/shell/man-explain.md).
const ManExplain = "EXPLAIN(1)                      Weft Commands                       EXPLAIN(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  explain — show how much of a pipeline is optimized\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    explain <pipeline>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  explain takes a one-liner, exactly as you would run it, and says which of its\n  stages are optimized and what would make more of them so. It does not run the\n  pipeline. When some stages are optimized and others are not, it runs one count\n  of the lines the optimized stages return, for the current range, and shows it\n  (see ROW COUNTS).\n\n  A pipeline is fastest when its stages are part of the optimized\n  retrieval: they narrow and reshape rows as the logs are fetched, so only the\n  result comes back to you. Any other stage runs post-retrieval, on every row\n  that came back. Each stage is reported as one of:\n\n    optimized retrieval     part of the retrieval; only its result comes back\n    partly optimized        its filter is optimized retrieval; the rest of the\n                            stage runs post-retrieval\n    post-retrieval          runs on rows that have already come back\n\n  The first stage that is not entirely optimized is the barrier. Every stage\n  after it runs post-retrieval, so the useful change is nearly always at or\n  before the barrier. A stage that is not optimized says why, and where there is\n  something to change, what to try.\n\n  When every stage is optimized retrieval, explain says so and there is nothing\n  to gain.\n\n  Relative paths resolve against the current directory, as they do for any\n  command. A script in /bin is explained through its body.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mROW COUNTS\x1b[0m\n  The lines that come back from the optimized retrieval are the lines every\n  post-retrieval stage has to read, so the count is what to weigh a barrier\n  against. It is printed above the first stage that is not entirely optimized:\n\n      1  grep \"query rpc: done\"   optimized retrieval\n      2  jq -r .msg               optimized retrieval\n           259,662 lines retrieved with optimization\n      3  awk '{ c = $4 }'         post-retrieval\n\n  With no optimized stage in front, it is the lines of the source itself\n  (962,932 lines retrieved).\n\n  The count is exact, as of now, and covers the pipeline's range, so it moves as\n  the range slides. It is a query of your own, so you must be able to read the\n  source, and it is cut off after a few seconds: when it can't be had explain\n  says line count unavailable and why, and the rest of the report is unchanged.\n  No post-retrieval stage is run to get it, and there is no count when every\n  stage is optimized, for a tail -f, or for a trace.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk '$3 == \"ingest-stream:\" { printf \"%s\\n\", $0 }' | grep 'first row'\n      1  awk '$3 == \"ingest-stream:\" { printf \"%s\\n\", $0 }'  partly optimized\n           pattern: optimized; action: post-retrieval\n           Action does more than print the line or its fields.\n           Try: Print only, e.g. `print $1, HOST`.\n      2  grep 'first row'                                    post-retrieval\n\n    Barrier: stage 1.\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep -n boom\n      1  grep -n boom  post-retrieval\n           grep optimized with -i, -v, or no flags; not -n.\n\n    Barrier: stage 1.\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep -c ERROR\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  In a terminal the placements are colored: green for optimized retrieval,\n  yellow for partly optimized, red for post-retrieval.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSAVED CRONS\x1b[0m\n  A cron runs on every fire, so saving one explains it automatically. Nothing is\n  printed when the whole pipeline is optimized retrieval; otherwise the save\n  succeeds and the same report follows it.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNOT SUPPORTED\x1b[0m\n  - Running the pipeline. explain shows no rows or timings, and the only count\n    is the retrieval's (see ROW COUNTS); use the pipeline itself for the rest.\n  - Options (explain -v, explain --json). They are rejected with\n    unknown option.\n  - Anything that is not a log query. explain echo hi is an error.\n"

// ManGit — git (generated from docs/shell/man-git.md).
const ManGit = "GIT(1)                          Weft Commands                           GIT(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  git — browse and restore earlier versions of scripts, crons and config\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    git log [--oneline] [-p] [--stat] [-n N] [<rev>] [-- <path>]\n    git show <rev>[:<path>]\n    git diff <a> <b> [-- <path>]\n    git blame <path>\n    git restore --source=<rev> <path>\n    git revert <rev>\n    git commit --amend -m <message>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  Every save to a script in /bin, a cron in /cron.d, a dashboard in\n  /etc/dashboards, or /etc/notify.yaml is captured in a short, browseable\n  history. You access that history and manipulate it with the git command.\n\n  Weft git is a very limited subset of true git behavior and is thus named\n  simply to support your muscle memory. Supported commands (git log, git\n  show, git diff, git blame git restore) behave as you'd expect, but\n  there's a lot not there by design. git --help lists what is supported.\n  Unsupported commands and unsupported flags are refused.\n\n  Committing is automatic and across your entire org's file structure. There is\n  no branching, no staging area, and no remotes. Therefore there are no merge or\n  rebase commands either.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLimits\x1b[0m\n  Weft keeps each file's latest six versions (the live one plus the\n  five before it). Older versions are deleted.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWhat's tracked\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mPath                         Tracked?\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------------------------  -------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  /bin/<name>                  ✓ — saves to scripts produce commits\n  /cron.d/<name>               ✓ — saves to crons produce commits\n  /etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml  ✓ — saves to dashboards produce commits\n  /etc/notify.yaml             ✓ — saves to the alert aliases produce\n                               commits\n  /etc/passwd, /etc/usage      ✗ — generated\n  /log/...                     ✗\n  /tmp/...                     ✗\n\n  The repository root is /, and tracked paths are bin/<name>,\n  cron.d/<name>, etc/dashboards/<name>.yaml and etc/notify.yaml.\n  Write a path any way git accepts it: /bin/scan,\n  bin/scan, or scan after cd /bin (after <rev>: use the\n  repo-relative form, bin/scan). A path outside the tracked set is refused: fatal: '/tmp/x' is outside the\n  repository (only /bin, /cron.d, /etc/dashboards and /etc/notify.yaml are tracked).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRevisions\x1b[0m\n  HEAD is the org's latest change. Use a path to narrow to a file (git log -- /bin/scan). Revisions work as in\n  git:\n\n  - HEAD, @, and the short hash git log prints (4 or more\n    characters).\n  - HEAD~N — N saves back. HEAD^ is HEAD~1. (HEAD^2 is the\n    second parent, as in git; this history has none, so it is an\n    error.)\n  - <a>..<b> — the commits in <b> that aren't in <a>.\n  - <rev>:<path> — a file's content as of a revision.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe oldest version\x1b[0m\n  The oldest version still kept for a file is shown the way a\n  shallow clone shows its edge: git log marks it grafted, its\n  patch shows the file as newly added, git blame prefixes its\n  lines with ^, and revisions older than that don't resolve\n  (fatal: bad revision). The cutoff is per file.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCommands\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit log\x1b[0m\n    weft> git log --oneline\n    a4fb0c2 (HEAD -> main) fix the awk filter\n    3d2e91f tighten error pattern\n    c01b745 Update cron.d/errors\n    1e8a02c initial pass\n\n  git log is the org's combined history; git log -- /bin/scan is\n  one file's. It takes --oneline, -p, --stat, -n N/-N,\n  --author=, --grep=, -S<text> (commits that added or removed\n  the text), --since=/--until= (\"2 days ago\", 2026-10-01),\n  --decorate/--no-decorate, --all, and a <rev> or\n  <a>..<b>. Anything else is refused:\n  fatal: unrecognized argument: --bogus.\n\n  A save with no message gets a generated one — Create bin/scan,\n  Update bin/scan, Delete bin/scan — because git requires a\n  message.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit show\x1b[0m\n    weft> git show HEAD~2:bin/scan\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n  git show <rev> prints a commit and its patch;\n  git show <rev>:<path> prints the file as it was then. Crons show\n  their # schedule: line first, so a version reads as a complete\n  cron file.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit diff\x1b[0m\n    weft> git diff HEAD~2 HEAD -- /bin/scan\n\n  git diff <a> <b> and git diff <a>..<b> compare two revisions,\n  optionally limited to paths after --. Plain git diff prints\n  nothing because the live file is HEAD.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit status\x1b[0m\n  Always On branch main / nothing to commit, working tree clean,\n  because the live file is HEAD.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit blame <path>\x1b[0m\n  Who last changed each line of a script or cron:\n\n    weft> git blame /bin/scan\n    ^1e8a02c (Eli Bergstrom 2026-04-22 17:33:12 +0000 1) cat /log/myco/api/main.log\n    a4fb0c2f (Dana Okafor 2026-04-25 09:42:01 +0000 2) | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"'\n\n  A ^ marks a line that comes from the oldest version still kept (the\n  file's first version, or the edge of the cap).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit restore, git checkout <rev> -- <path>\x1b[0m\n  Make an older version current again:\n\n    weft> git restore --source=HEAD~3 /bin/scan\n    weft> git checkout HEAD~3 -- /bin/scan        # same thing, older spelling\n\n  This writes the old content as a new commit (Restore bin/scan\n  from 0174c77) — history is never rewritten — through the same path as vi: your permissions, the\n  Lua/bash classification, and (for crons) your plan's schedule\n  limits all apply. It also brings back a script that was deleted,\n  if its history is still there. git checkout <rev> without --\n  is refused.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit revert <rev>\x1b[0m\n  Undo one commit with a new commit. If the file changed again since\n  that commit, git's conflict is reported and nothing is written:\n\n    error: could not revert 3d2e91f... tighten error pattern\n    hint: bin/scan has changed since that commit, so the revert would conflict; nothing was written\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit commit --amend -m <message>\x1b[0m\n  Rewords the latest commit. You can reword only a file's current\n  version, only one you wrote yourself, and only with permission to\n  edit that file — so you can't reword a teammate's save that landed\n  after yours. A plain git commit reports nothing to commit.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mgit rev-parse\x1b[0m\n  git rev-parse HEAD, --short, --abbrev-ref HEAD, --verify\n  — handy inside /bin scripts.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mColour and pipes\x1b[0m\n  Output that goes straight to your terminal is coloured. Output\n  headed into a pipe or a redirect is plain text, so\n  git log -p | grep foo and git show HEAD:bin/scan > /tmp/scan\n  work as in a shell. --color=always or --color=never overrides.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCommit messages at save time\x1b[0m\n  Right after a successful save in vi of a file git tracks (a script,\n  cron, dashboard or notify.yaml), the shell drops you into a\n  follow-up vi buffer pre-filled with a comment-prefixed prompt:\n\n    # Commit message for /bin/scan.\n    # One or more lines; the first line is the subject shown in `git log`.\n    # Save & exit (:wq) to record. Quit without saving (:q!) to skip.\n\n    █\n\n  Type a message, :wq records it. :q! skips it. Either way you\n  end up back at the shell prompt. Comment lines (#-prefixed) and\n  blank lines are stripped; an entirely-comment buffer records no\n  message. To add one later, use git commit --amend -m.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDeleting and bringing back\x1b[0m\n  Deleting a script or cron shows up as a Delete bin/<name>\n  commit, and git restore --source=<rev> <path> brings it back\n  with its history intact. Saving a new file over a deleted name does\n  the same thing: the deletion stays in the log, followed by the new\n  save.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mPermissions\x1b[0m\n  git log, show, diff, status, blame and rev-parse are\n  read-only — the same access as cat /bin/foo or cat /cron.d/foo.\n\n  git restore, checkout -- <path>, revert and commit --amend\n  are writes. They need the same group as editing the file does:\n  binmgr for /bin, cronmgr for /cron.d, configmgr for /etc.\n  Restoring an old dashboard still validates it: if it refers to a cron\n  that has since been deleted, the restore is refused.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mUnsupported commands\x1b[0m\n  - Working tree and staging. add, rm, mv, stash,\n    reset, clean are refused (is a git command, but weft doesn't\n    support it); every write is already a commit.\n  - Branches, merges and rebases. History is one linear sequence; these are refused.\n  - Remotes. clone, fetch, pull, push are refused.\n  - /tmp, /etc/passwd and /etc/usage. These keep no history.\n    /tmp is ephemeral; the other two are generated from group\n    membership and metering. Change the team with adduser / deluser.\n  - Slack setup. Only the alias file /etc/notify.yaml is versioned;\n    connecting Slack creates no commit.\n  - Patterns are RE2. --author and --grep use Go\n    regular expressions, so +, ? and | are operators.\n  - Flags beyond the ones listed. Each command takes only the flags\n    named above. git log has no --format, --pretty, --follow,\n    --name-only or -L, and takes one revision. git diff takes only\n    --cached/--staged, so no --stat or --name-only. git status,\n    git show and git blame take none (no --short, no -L).\n    git restore takes --source/-s and --worktree/-W, not\n    --staged. git commit takes --amend and -m, and cannot make a new\n    commit. git revert takes --no-edit and one commit. Only\n    --color=always|never and --no-color apply everywhere. A refused flag\n    reports unrecognized argument, unknown option or invalid option,\n    as git does.\n"

// ManLess — less (generated from docs/shell/man-less.md).
const ManLess = "LESS(1)                         Weft Commands                          LESS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  less — page through output one screen at a time\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    <pipeline> | less\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  An in-process pager for weft. It runs inside the shell and behaves\n  the same in the web shell and the native CLI.\n\n  When a pipeline ends with | less the query runs to completion,\n  the result is loaded into the pager, and the shell shows\n  the pager until you press q.\n\n  Help and man pages auto-page when their output would overflow\n  the viewport — press q to return to the prompt.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAVIGATION\x1b[0m\n    space, Page Down, f, Ctrl+F   forward one screen\n    b, Page Up, Ctrl+B            back one screen\n    j, Down                       forward one line\n    k, Up                         back one line\n    g, gg                         jump to top\n    G                             jump to bottom\n    q, Ctrl+C                     exit the pager\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEARCH\x1b[0m\n    /pattern    search forward (case-sensitive substring)\n    n           next match\n    N           previous match\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSTATUS LINE\x1b[0m\n  Bottom row shows the source title on the left and a percent\n  position on the right. Incremental search and error messages\n  replace the status briefly.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNOT SUPPORTED\x1b[0m\n  - Command-line options. less does not read them: -N, -S, -R, -i,\n    -F and the rest are accepted and have no effect.\n  - Backward search (?), case-insensitive or regular-expression search; /\n    is a case-sensitive substring match.\n  - Numeric counts (5j), half-page scrolling (d, u), follow mode (F),\n    opening the text in an editor (v) and marks.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help, man alert, man range\n"

// ManPasswd — passwd (generated from docs/shell/man-passwd.md).
const ManPasswd = "PASSWD(5)                       Weft Commands                        PASSWD(5)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  passwd — the org team roster and its group-based permissions\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    cat /etc/passwd\n    adduser <email> [<group>]\n    deluser <email> [<group>]\n    groups [<email>]\n    id [<email>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  A weft org's members and their permissions are modeled as Unix-style\n  group membership. /etc/passwd is the read-only roster — one row per\n  member with their role, status, and groups:\n\n    USER             ROLE     STATUS    GROUPS\n    alice@acme.com   admin    active    binmgr  cronmgr  configmgr  nsmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    bob@acme.com     member   active    binmgr  cronmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    carol@acme.com   member   invited   —\n\n  Change the team with adduser and deluser (admin-only), or from\n  the dashboard's Members tab. An admin (sometimes\n  called the org owner) holds groups like any other member and is listed\n  with them.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGROUPS\x1b[0m\n  A group is one of these:\n\n  - binmgr / cronmgr / configmgr — edit access to /bin,\n    /cron.d, and /etc respectively.\n  - nsmgr — create and manage namespaces.\n  - <namespace> — read (query) the logs under /log/<namespace>.\n  - <namespace>+tokens — mint producer tokens for that namespace.\n    Independent of read: a member can have one, the other, or both.\n\n  The +tokens facet uses + because + is illegal in a namespace\n  name, so it can never collide with a real namespace.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCOMMANDS\x1b[0m\n  - adduser <email> — invite someone to the org. Sends an invite\n    email; they join when they accept (or next sign in).\n  - adduser <email> <group> — add a member to a group. If the email\n    isn't on the team yet, they're invited now (the email goes out) and\n    the grant applies after they accept, so re-run the command once\n    they've joined. If they were invited but haven't accepted, the\n    command returns an error.\n  - deluser <email> <group> — revoke a group membership.\n  - deluser <email> — remove a member from the org entirely.\n  - groups [<email>] — list a member's groups (yours if omitted).\n    Prints <email>: group group ...; an email not on the team gives\n    no such member: <email>.\n  - id [<email>] — show a member's role and groups, as\n    uid=<email> role=<role> groups=a,b.\n\n  Every command takes --help (prints usage, changes nothing). An unknown\n  flag, a first argument that isn't an email address, or extra arguments\n  is a usage error.\n\n  adduser/deluser are admin-only. Group grants apply once the\n  member has accepted their invite.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNOT SUPPORTED\x1b[0m\n  These manage org members, not operating-system accounts.\n\n  - Passwords, login shells, home directories and the usual adduser /\n    useradd options (-G, -u, -s, --disabled-password).\n  - Several emails or several groups in one call. adduser and deluser\n    take one email and at most one group.\n  - Creating a group of your own. Groups are the fixed set above plus one\n    per namespace.\n  - Editing /etc/passwd; it is a read-only roster.\n  - Looking up anyone by name, uid or anything other than an email address.\n\n  Other options are rejected with <command>: unknown option <flag> and the\n  command's usage line.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    adduser dana@acme.com                  # invite\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments         # let dana read payments logs\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments+tokens  # ...and mint producer tokens\n    adduser dana@acme.com cronmgr          # ...and edit /cron.d\n    deluser dana@acme.com payments         # revoke read\n    deluser dana@acme.com                  # remove from the org\n    groups dana@acme.com\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSEE ALSO\x1b[0m\n  help builtins, help index\n"

// ManRange — range (generated from docs/shell/man-range.md).
const ManRange = "RANGE(1)                        Weft Commands                         RANGE(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNAME\x1b[0m\n  range — display or set the log query time range\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSYNOPSIS\x1b[0m\n    range\n    range <start>..<end>\n    range <start>..\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDESCRIPTION\x1b[0m\n  With no arguments, range displays the current query time window.\n\n  With a <start>..<end> argument, range sets the time window used\n  by all subsequent queries. If <end> is omitted, it defaults to\n  now. Relative specs are re-resolved against the server's clock on\n  every query — range 1d.. always means \"the last 24 hours\".\n\n  Each side of the range can be:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mABSOLUTE TIMESTAMP\x1b[0m\n  A calendar date or instant. Missing components default to the\n  start of the period: an omitted time means 00:00:00, an omitted\n  day means the 1st, an omitted month means January. An omitted\n  year defaults to the current year, so 01-15 in March means\n  current-year January.\n  Server-local timezone unless the literal carries one.\n\n    2026-04-12T10:30:00Z    full RFC3339 with timezone\n    2026-04-12T10:30:00     second resolution\n    2026-04-12T10:30        minute resolution\n    2026-04-12T10           hour resolution\n    2026-04-12              day resolution\n    2026-04                 month resolution\n    2026                    year resolution\n    04-12T10:30             current-year shorthand with time\n    04-12                   current-year shorthand, midnight\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRELATIVE DURATION\x1b[0m\n  Distance back from \"now\". Re-anchored against the server's clock\n  on every query, so a long-lived shell pane always queries a current\n  window. Units are m (minutes), h (hours) and d (days);\n  seconds, weeks and combined forms such as 1h30m are not accepted.\n\n    30m                     30 minutes ago\n    2h                      2 hours ago\n    7d                      7 days ago\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mNAMED REFERENCES\x1b[0m\n    now                     current time\n    today                   midnight today\n    yesterday               midnight yesterday\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mEXAMPLES\x1b[0m\n    range                   show current range\n    range 2h..              last 2 hours\n    range 30m..             last 30 minutes\n    range 7d..              last 7 days\n    range 1h..10m           from 1 hour ago to 10 minutes ago\n    range yesterday..today  all of yesterday\n    range yesterday..       yesterday to now\n    range 04-01..05-14      current-year span, Apr 1 00:00 up to\n                            (excluding) May 14 00:00\n    range 05-14..           from May 14 midnight to now\n    range 05-14T09..05-14T17\n                            business hours on May 14 (current year)\n    range 2026-04-01..2026-04-12\n                            explicit-year date range\n    range 2026-04-12T10:00..2026-04-12T11:00\n                            specific hour\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNOT SUPPORTED\x1b[0m\n  - Seconds, weeks and combined durations (30s, 1w, 1h30m): use m,\n    h and d.\n  - A bare start with no .. (range 2h): the .. is required, and the\n    start must be before the end.\n  - A range for a single command. range sets the window for every query\n    that follows; there is no per-command --since.\n  - Options of any kind (range -h, range --reset). Anything that is not a\n    <start>..<end> spec is rejected with an error and leaves the current\n    range unchanged.\n"

// HelpAlerts — Alerts (generated from docs/manual/alerts.md).
const HelpAlerts = "  The alert function generates notifications based on stdin and other\n  arguments. This page provides a reference for the function, a cookbook of\n  alerting strategies, and how to route notifications.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSynopsis\x1b[0m\n    <pipeline> | alert [<condition>] [--notify=<alias>]\n                       [-v <field>] [-t|--tag <field>]\n                       [--duration <dur>] [--average] [--msg <text>]\n\n    <pipeline> | alert (--if-zero <v> | --if-nonzero <v>) [--notify=<alias>]\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mHow alerts work\x1b[0m\n  alert reads a value from stdin and evaluates it against a condition. If the\n  condition holds, it writes the alert message to stderr and, when --notify is\n  supplied, dispatches the alias from /etc/notify.yaml to its Slack, PagerDuty,\n  webhook, and/or email targets (see Notifications). Its value\n  and tag flags can serve as a shorthand for shaping what gets persisted in a\n  cron (man crons).\n\n  With no condition the default is any: the alert fires whenever stdin is\n  non-empty. alert alone is a dry run that writes to stderr.\n\n  alert must be the last stage of its pipeline, and the last (or only) command\n  of a script.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConditions\x1b[0m\n  - any — fires whenever stdin is non-empty (the default when no condition is given)\n  - above <N> — current value is greater than N\n  - below <N> — current value is less than N\n  - eq <N> — current value equals N\n  - neq <N> — current value does not equal N\n  - changed — value differs from previous run\n  - absent — no input received in the window\n  - rising <N>% — value increased by N% compared to previous/window average\n  - falling <N>% — value decreased by N% compared to previous/window average\n  - matches <regex> — value matches a regular expression (RE2)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOptions\x1b[0m\n  - -v <field>, --value <field> — column or JSON path holding the number to\n    compare on each line. If unspecified, the whole input must parse as a float,\n    unless the condition is any, absent and changed.\n  - -t <field>, --tag <field> — column or JSON path by which alerts should be\n    grouped. Combine with -v when you want to alert separately by host, for\n    example.\n\n  In a cron, -v and -t obviate the need for the # value:/# tag:\n  headers (man crons) a cron without alert would use. A cron that\n  alerts on tagged lines or JSON must give -v; without it, output will fail\n  runtime type checks (man crons) and automatically be disabled.\n  If the stdin to alert is typed as text (man crons), the condition\n  must be one of any, absent, changed and matches.\n\n  - --duration <dur> — time window for sustained conditions. For\n    above/below, condition must hold for all runs in the window. For\n    rising/falling, compares against the window average. For absent, no\n    non-empty output in the window. Units are s, m and h only.\n  - --average — for above/below with --duration, compare the rolling\n    average over the window against the threshold. No effect without\n    --duration.\n  - --msg <text> — override the default alert message.\n  - --notify=<alias> — dispatch to the named alias in /etc/notify.yaml (see\n    Notifications). Without --notify, alert writes to stderr.\n  - --if-zero <status> / --if-nonzero <status> — fire based on a shell exit\n    status instead of a stdin value. Pass the status of the command just before\n    alert as \"$?\". --if-zero fires when the status is 0, --if-nonzero is\n    the inverse. Mutually exclusive with the condition keywords. Useful in longer\n    scripts.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mExamples\x1b[0m\n    # Alert if the range holds any ERROR line.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 0 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert when the range holds more than 50 errors.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=critical\n\n    # check_errors is a script in /bin that prints a numeric\n    check_errors | alert above 50 --duration 5m --average --notify=ops\n    check_errors | alert rising 30% --notify=critical\n\n    # Alert when the range holds no canary line at all.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/canary/main.log | wc -l | alert eq 0 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert when a lease changes hands: a text cron, where lease-holders is a\n    # /bin script printing \"<holder> <lease>\" lines.\n    #   # schedule: */2 * * * *\n    #   # type: text\n    lease-holders | alert changed -v 1 -t 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # Page when any host boots a release candidate; the cron still stores every\n    # host's build.\n    #   # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    #   # type: text\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep 'starting version' \\\n        | awk '{v[HOST]=$NF} END {for (h in v) print v[h], h}' \\\n        | alert matches '-rc[0-9]*$' -v 1 -t 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # One page if any host's disk use is above 80%.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n        | awk '{print HOST, $5}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v 2 --notify=ops\n\n    # Alert per host whose disk use is above 80%.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log \\\n        | awk '{print HOST, $5}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # Same idea with JSON rows\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/disk.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct}' \\\n        | alert above 80 -v .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLayering crons for richer alerts\x1b[0m\n  A cron run, and thereby an alert reads only the logs from its own schedule\n  interval: a */5 cron sees the last five minutes. However, you can leverage\n  the stored results of other crons using the weft::* predicates.\n\n  The simplest layer reads another cron's history with an aggregate and pipes\n  the number to alert:\n\n    # /cron.d/error-rate-hourly\n    # schedule: every 5m\n    weft::avg error-rate 1h | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  If you want to use boolean logic to add multiple such predicates, just add one\n  more condition and echo if nothing's failed yet:\n\n    # /cron.d/deploy-danger\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    (weft::is_above error-rate 50 && weft::is_absent canary-ping && echo 1) | alert --notify=critical\n\n  If your love of bash is less than it should be, you may want to stuff a Lua\n  script in /bin and pipe that to alert.\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    local rate = 0\n    if not weft.is_absent(\"error-rate\") then\n      rate = tonumber(weft.latest(\"error-rate\")) or 0\n    end\n    local canary_dead = weft.is_absent(\"canary-ping\")\n    local in_maintenance = not weft.is_absent(\"maintenance-flag\")\n\n    if rate > 50 and canary_dead and not in_maintenance then\n      io.stderr:write(string.format(\"composite fire: rate=%.1f canary_dead=%s\\n\",\n        rate, tostring(canary_dead)))\n      print(1)\n    else\n      print(0)\n    end\n\n  We'll write that script to /bin/deploy_danger, and then we can write this for\n  our alerting cron:\n\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    deploy_danger | alert above 0 --notify=critical\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mStatic analysis\x1b[0m\n  In addition to the checks run for every cron (man crons), there are some specific checks run related to crons that contain alert invocations:\n\n  - Alert positioning -- alert can only be invoked once per cron, and it\n    has to be the last thing executed\n  - Multiple aliases -- alert will bail if you provide --notify more than\n    once (if you want several targets for an alert, you can configure your alias\n    with as many as you like)\n  - Bad arguments -- all conditions and flags must be known and stdin,\n    positional arguments, and flag arguments must typecheck correctly, and -v\n    and -t must not mix numeric field positions with a JSON path\n  - One schema -- a cron that alerts must declare its value and tagging with\n    -v/-t, not # value:/# tag: headers\n  - Types -- on a cron with non-numeric\n    output (man crons), the condition must be any,\n    absent, changed or matches, and matches must not receive numeric input\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNotifications\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDefine a notification alias\x1b[0m\n  When alert executes in a cron, --notify=<alias> dispatches to a destination\n  defined in /etc/notify.yaml. One alias specifies one or more targets across\n  four service types — Slack, PagerDuty, webhook, and email.\n\n    ops:\n      slack:\n        - \"#alerts\"\n      pagerduty:\n        - ops-daytime\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n\n  Edit it /etc/notify.yaml with vim in the weft shell.  Weft refuses to save\n  /etc/notify.yaml if:\n\n  - the file doesn't parse\n  - an alias has no targets\n  - a webhook url or header refers to a ${secrets.NAME} you haven't defined\n  - you removed the system alias\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mThe system alias\x1b[0m\n  Every org has an alias named system. It receives the alerts weft raises, as\n  opposed to the ones your own alert stages raise. For example, weft pages\n  system when a cron stops executing successfully, or when it disables a cron\n  after runtime analysis failures (man crons). Weft initially\n  populates it with the email address of the person who created the org:\n\n    system:\n      email:\n        - \"you@example.com\"\n\n  You can change its target to any service type. You just can't remove the\n  top-level key.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mConnecting Slack and PagerDuty\x1b[0m\n  notify.yaml names destinations — the credentials behind them live with your\n  account. Connect them  under Account → Integrations in the browser or from\n  the terminal with weft integration.\n\n    # PagerDuty\n    weft integration pagerduty add ops-daytime --key YOUR_ROUTING_KEY\n    weft integration pagerduty rm  ops-daytime\n\n    # Slack\n    weft integration slack connect\n    weft integration slack rm\n\n  A PagerDuty alias in notify.yaml must match a name you've registered with\n  weft integration pagerduty add. Slack channels are named directly in\n  notify.yaml (\"#alerts\") once the workspace is connected, and you'll have to\n  add weft to those channels for it to notify you.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWebhooks\x1b[0m\n  A webhook target is either a bare URL or a mapping with a url and optional\n  headers:\n\n    pages:\n      webhook:\n        - \"https://hooks.example.com/weft\"\n        - url: \"https://api.example.com/alerts\"\n          headers:\n            Authorization: \"Bearer ${secrets.pages_token}\"\n            X-Team: ops\n\n  When an alert opens an incident, weft sends each webhook target one HTTP POST\n  whose body is JSON:\n\n    {\n      \"v\": 2,\n      \"cron\": \"disk-use[web-1]\",\n      \"alias\": \"pages\",\n      \"ts_unix\": 1792677600,\n      \"body\": \"disk-use[web-1]: 91.0 > 80.0\"\n    }\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField    Value\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ---------------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  v        The version of the weft webhooks protocol.\n  cron     The name of the cron that alerted. With alert -t, the tag is appended\n           in brackets, as above, so each tag has its own name.\n  alias    The notify.yaml alias that routed the alert\n  ts_unix  When weft sent the request, in seconds since the epoch\n  body     The alert message: what the alert prints to stderr, or your --msg if\n           you gave one. At most 1024 bytes, with ... (truncated) appended when\n           it was cut\n\n  You bring your own HTTP headers, with the exception of Content-Type:\n  application/json, which you can override should you need to.\n\n  A webhook succeeds when it answers with a status below 300 within the\n  delivery timeout. Anything else is a failed delivery and\n  is retried on the following ticks of the incident.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWebhook secrets\x1b[0m\n  If you need to use a token in an auth header for a webhook but would rather not\n  shove a secret somewhere broadly readable, store it as a webhook secret instead\n  and refer to it by name:\n\n    weft integration webhook-secret set my_token --value s3cr3t\n\n  You can then interpolate it with ${secrets.my_token} in a webhook's url or\n  in a header value, as in the example above. Weft substitutes the value when it\n  sends the request.  Error messages, logs, catting /etc/notify.yaml etc. show\n  ${secrets.pages_token} instead of the value.\n\n  Secret names must be [\\w_-]*. You can't write notify.yaml if it refers to a\n  secret you haven't defined, and weft integration webhook-secret rm <name>\n  will fail if an alias still refers to it.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mEmail\x1b[0m\n  Email addresses carry no account-level credential. They are written into\n  notify.yaml per alias:\n\n    pages:\n      email:\n        - \"oncall@example.com\"\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTesting an alias\x1b[0m\n    echo \"test fire\" | alert --notify=ops\n\n  This sends to every target in the ops alias.\n\n  Interactive runs notify every time. Only crons have incidents, so nothing is\n  suppressed.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mKey behaviors\x1b[0m\n  A cron checks its condition on every tick. To avoid pagestorming you, weft\n  groups consecutive ticks during which the condition holds. A cron will only\n  notify once per entrance into a failed state (or per tag if specified).  If a\n  send fails (PagerDuty is unreachable, a Slack channel is gone), the run reports\n  it and exits non-zero, and each following tick of the incident tries again.\n  Delivery is tracked per target, so a retry goes only to the targets that missed\n  it, not to the ones already told.  The first tick where the condition doesn't\n  hold sets it to green. It won't autoresolve in PagerDuty when it does so.\n\n  Each target gets 10 seconds to accept a notification, regardless of service.  A\n  target that doesn't is flagged as failed, and three consecutive failed\n  deliveries disable the cron.\n"

// ManualCli — The weft CLI (generated from docs/manual/cli.md).
const ManualCli = "CLI(1)                          Weft Commands                           CLI(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe weft CLI\x1b[0m\n  Weft has a the command-line client. It's called weft. It opens the\n  shell (man introduction) where you query your logs, and it manages the things\n  around them: namespaces, tokens, sources and integrations. Anything you can do\n  in the browser, you can do from here. Sometimes the opposite is not true, so\n  get used to reaching for the executable in the terminal.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mInstallation\x1b[0m\n  Download the binary for your platform (Linux or macOS, amd64 or arm64) from the\n  Downloads page, or fetch it directly:\n\n    curl -Lo weft https://releases.w3ft.net/weft/latest/weft-darwin-arm64\n    chmod +x weft && sudo mv weft /usr/local/bin/\n\n  Swap darwin-arm64 for darwin-amd64, linux-amd64 or linux-arm64. Check\n  it with weft version.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAuthenticating\x1b[0m\n  weft login prints a URL and a short code, and opens the URL in your browser.\n  Enter the code there and approve the request; the CLI waits, then saves your\n  credentials.\n\n  Credentials are saved to ~/.weft.yaml, which holds the API endpoint, your\n  token and your active org. A .weft.yaml in the current directory, if there is\n  one, is read instead of it -- handy if you use weft with multiple\n  organizations. weft whoami shows who you're logged in as, your team and your\n  plan.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mCommands\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                                 What it does\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft sh [-c 'pipeline'] [--range        Open the shell (man introduction), or\n  <start>..<end>]                         run one pipeline and exit\n  weft login                              Authenticate through your browser\n  weft whoami                             Show your user, team, and plan\n  weft org [list | switch <name>]         List the orgs you belong to or switch\n                                          the active one\n  weft namespace {list | create |         Manage namespaces (man namespaces)\n  remove}\n  weft token {create | list | revoke}     Manage tokens (man namespaces)\n  weft source {add | list | remove |      Manage log sources and their schemas\n  pending | configure | resample |        (man overview)\n  migrate | schema}\n  weft integration {list | slack |        Connect notification (help alerts)\n  pagerduty}                              destinations\n  weft mcp                                Run an MCP server on stdio, so an LLM\n                                          client such as Claude Code can read\n                                          your logs and propose scripts and\n                                          alerting crons\n  weft propose {review | apply | clear}   Review, apply or discard the changes\n                                          an MCP client has staged\n  weft update                             Replace the binary with the latest\n                                          release\n  weft version                            Print the version\n\n  Run weft <command> --help for the details of any command.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOptions for every command\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mOption               Effect\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------  ---------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  --org NAME, -o NAME  Use this org for the one invocation instead of the saved\n                       one.\n  --nocolor            Turn off colored output. Color is also off when the\n                       output is piped, and NO_COLOR is honored.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mEnvironment variables\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mVariable    Effect\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----------  ------------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  WEFT_TOKEN  Use this token instead of the one in .weft.yaml.\n  WEFT_ORG    Use this org instead of the one in .weft.yaml. --org wins over it.\n"

// ManualConnectCloudwatch — CloudWatch (generated from docs/manual/cloudwatch.md).
const ManualConnectCloudwatch = "CONNECT-CLOUDWATCH(1)           Weft Commands            CONNECT-CLOUDWATCH(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a CloudWatch source\x1b[0m\n  If your logs already land in AWS CloudWatch Logs (Lambda, ECS/Fargate, API\n  Gateway, RDS, etc.), you can deploy a small CloudFormation stack in your own\n  AWS account that forwards a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter through a\n  Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream to weft.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mCreate a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces). There's nothing special about Cloudwatch\n  that requires its own token, but it's probably tidier if it's got its own.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRegister the source\x1b[0m\n  Use the weft CLI to tell weft which log group maps to which service. For\n  example, if we wanted to add the service lovelace to the loom namespace:\n\n    weft source add loom/lovelace \\\n      --log-group /aws/lambda/lovelace \\\n      --region us-east-2\n\n  The source will now show up as pending in weft source list or weft source\n  pending until you get logs flowing.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDeploy the CloudFormation stack\x1b[0m\n  In the AWS account and region that own the log group, deploy the Firehose\n  stack. Create one stack per account and region you want to connect using the\n  following 1-liner:\n\n    aws cloudformation create-stack \\\n      --stack-name weft-cloudwatch-firehose \\\n      --template-url https://weft-public.s3.amazonaws.com/cfn/weft-cloudwatch-firehose.yaml \\\n      --parameters ParameterKey=WeftToken,ParameterValue=<your-weft-namespace-token> \\\n                   ParameterKey=LogGroupNames,ParameterValue=\"/aws/lambda/lovelace\" \\\n      --capabilities CAPABILITY_IAM\n\n  LogGroupNames is a comma-separated list, so one stack can subscribe several\n  log groups in the same account/region.\n\n  Running the above will create the following resources:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mResource                                Purpose\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream   Receives your log events from\n                                          CloudWatch Logs and POSTs them over\n                                          HTTPS to weft's ingest endpoint,\n                                          authenticated with your token. It\n                                          buffers up to 1 MB or 60 seconds,\n                                          whichever comes first, and retries a\n                                          failed delivery for up to 5 minutes.\n  S3 bucket                               Holds only the records weft's endpoint\n                                          couldn't accept, gzipped under failed/\n                                          and errors/, and deletes them after 30\n                                          days (Firehose requires one for this\n                                          kind of destination)\n  CloudWatch log group                    The delivery stream's own\n  /aws/kinesisfirehose/weft-<stack name>  delivery-attempt logs, kept for 14\n                                          days\n  IAM role for Firehose                   Lets Firehose write that log group and\n                                          the S3 bucket\n  IAM role for CloudWatch Logs            Lets CloudWatch Logs put records on\n                                          this stream, assumable only by log\n                                          groups in this account and region\n  Lambda function and its IAM role        Small function that runs when the\n                                          stack is created, updated or deleted.\n                                          It maintains subscription filters\n                                          named weft for all currently log\n                                          groups.\n  Per-log-group subscription filter       Forwards every event in that group to\n                                          the delivery stream (created by the\n                                          Lambda above, don't appear as stack\n                                          resources)\n\n  To add or remove log groups, update the stack's LogGroupNames parameter and\n  redeploy the stack.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mApprove a schema\x1b[0m\n  Once your log group starts emitting events, weft accumulates a sample from that\n  traffic and the source goes from sampling to pending. If you want nice,\n  speedy lookups, approve a\n  schema (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mFinding your logs\x1b[0m\n  Weft maps each registered CloudWatch log group to one service, and every event\n  in it lands in that service's main.log:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCloudWatch log group  Registered as  weft file\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------  -------------  ---------------------------\x1b[0m\n  /aws/lambda/lovelace  loom/lovelace  /log/loom/lovelace/main.log\n  /ecs/myapp/web        loom/web       /log/loom/web/main.log\n\n  Each row carries the event's own CloudWatch timestamp, so rows sort by when the\n  event happened, not when it arrived. Rows have no instance, because the\n  CloudWatch log stream name isn't kept. Events from a log group you haven't\n  registered with weft source add are dropped.\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n"

// ManualOverview — Overview (generated from docs/manual/connect-source.md).
const ManualOverview = "OVERVIEW(1)                     Weft Commands                      OVERVIEW(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a log source\x1b[0m\n  The process for connecting a log source to weft is mostly the same regardless\n  of whether you're using our own agent, pushing batches over HTTPS, or streaming\n  syslog over TCP:\n\n  1. Create a token in the namespace of your choosing as described in Namespaces\n     & tokens (man namespaces);\n  2. Feed that token to your log shipping mechanism;\n  3. Configure a schema to extract useful bits of information from your log lines\n     (optional for source types we don't control; with our own agent, we enforce\n     this part).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSupported producers\x1b[0m\n  - weftd (man weftd) is our homegrown log shipping agent. It's the fastest\n    option and has a few more bells and whistles but you have to be able to\n    actually run it -- not possible in every environment.\n  - Cloudwatch+Kinesis (man connect-cloudwatch) -- our recommended path for monitoring\n    AWS Lambdas.\n  - Heroku (man heroku) -- hey, Heroku is actually still in business, and we've\n    still got some applications running there, so we got to support!\n  - Render (man connect-render) -- our first foray into the new batch of Heroku successors.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSampling logs for a schema\x1b[0m\n  Once a source starts producing lines, you'll see it in the \"sources\" under its\n  respective namespace. It will start off in a \"sampling\" state -- we capture a\n  50-line chunk to help to define a schema for that source. You can watch that\n  progress in the browser or with weft source pending --watch.\n\n  When using weftd, you're forced to define a schema for your log lines, (unless\n  the lines were written by a weft SDK, in which case then timestamp, level,\n  trace ID and spans come from each line's header). Though you don't have to\n  define a schema for other producers, we highly recommend it.  Having a means of\n  plucking metavariables (man metavars) out of the logs lets us make lookups on\n  those values nice and snappy.\n\n  Once your log source has transitioned from \"sampling\" to \"pending,\" you can\n  view our best guess about your sample in the browser or on the CLI with weft\n  source configure. A little wizard will walk you through our guesses for each\n  of the values we want to extract and you can approve, reject, etc.\n\n  You can resample at any time by clicking \"resample\" or running weft source\n  resample.\n"

// ManualCrons — Crons (generated from docs/manual/crons.md).
const ManualCrons = "CRONS(1)                        Weft Commands                         CRONS(1)\n\n  In weft, crons have two primary purposes: generating summary timeseries data\n  based on logs and metrics, and alerting on such data. Creating such a cron is\n  as simple as adding a bash script to /cron.d. Those scripts need a leading\n  comment indicating the schedule on which the cron will fire.\n\n  Let's take the following script as an example:\n\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mScheduling\x1b[0m\n  There are two options for cron schedule syntax: OG crontab syntax, and an\n  interval syntax. Crons fire on fixed intervals at round UTC times. Below are\n  the accepted values for each:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mcrontab      interval\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------  ----------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  * * * * *    every minute\n  */N * * * *  every N minutes\n  0 * * * *    every hour\n  0 */H * * *  every H hours\n  0 0 * * *    every day, at 00:00 UTC\n  0 0 */N * *  every N days\n  0 0 * * 1    every week, on Monday at 00:00 UTC\n\n  Weft will refuse to save a file in /cron.d if the schedule line is missing or\n  fails to conform to the scheduling options above.\n\n  Weft sets the range for each run automatically based on the cron's schedule\n  interval: a */5 cron sees the last five minutes, a daily cron sees the UTC\n  day that just ended, and a weekly cron sees the week that just ended.  For this\n  reason, we recommend against using head -n, tail -n, etc.  Imposing a limit\n  on your lines might resulting in undercounting or other missed line problems.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCron result persistence\x1b[0m\n  Since your cron monitors are just bash/Lua scripts against your logs, it's very\n  easy to test them out since you have a shell that executes in the exact same\n  way. But once you are done tweaking and get them saved to /cron.d, every cron\n  run gets persisted. After they've been running for a bit, you'll have a\n  portrait of what the runs look like over time.\n\n    weft::describe error-rate | jq -r 'select(.exit_code != 0) | .ran_at + \" \" + .stderr'\n\n  The output of this function is NDJSON with the following values:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField        Contents\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------  -----------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  stdout       The line the point came from\n  stderr       Alert firings (from an alert stage), or why the run failed or\n               struck\n  exit_code    0, or 1 if the run failed or struck\n  duration_ms  How long the run took, in milliseconds\n  ran_at       When the run started\n  value        The point's value, empty for a text cron\n  text         A text cron (man crons)'s value, absent otherwise\n  tag          The point's tag, empty if untagged\n\n  A run that prints several points has one entry per point. A run that fails\n  has a single entry, with no value, with the reason in stderr.\n\n  We'll cover more ways of analyzing cron data below.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mType declarations\x1b[0m\n  Crons have a simple type system that helps weft guide your scripts. Whatever\n  can be enforced statically is, but much of the type checking is done at\n  runtime. By default, a cron is assumed to return one numeric value per line,\n  with an optional tag following whitespace. You can declare richer types with\n  comments at the top of the file. With or without explicit type declarations, a\n  cron that fails runtime analysis will be automatically disabled. Here's how\n  weft analyzes stdout for a cron with no type declarations:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mLine            Outcome\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------  -------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  42              value 42 is stored, no tag\n  42 api-1        value 42 is stored, tagged api-1\n  42 api-1 extra  fails analysis for having more than two fields, cron disabled\n  n/a api-1       fails analysis for non-numeric first field, cron disabled\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDeclaring text output\x1b[0m\n  If you've got a series of strings in your logs you want to roll up in some way,\n  you can, you just have to tell weft that you're expecting text. For example:\n\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    # type: text\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep 'starting version' \\\n        | awk '{v[HOST]=$NF} END {for (h in v) print v[h], h}'\n\n  Downstream of this cron, alert, chart, dashboard, and aggregate functions\n  will do typechecking and disallow certain modes based on this type declaration.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mExtracting values and tags from JSON\x1b[0m\n  If the stdout from your cron is JSON, you need to tell weft what you'd like\n  persisted in the value and tag fields. You can do so with comments\n  indicating the JSON path:\n\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    # value: .pct\n    # tag: .host\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}'\n\n  A type: text declaration applies to whatever JSON path you've pointed value\n  at if you want to extract a string from an object.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mPlan limits on crons\x1b[0m\n  Number of executing crons, scheduling granularity, and tag count are limited by your plan:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mPlan    Shortest interval  Crons  Tags per run\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------  -----------------  -----  ------------\x1b[0m\n  Trial   5 minutes          1      10\n  Swatch  5 minutes          20     50\n  Yard    1 minute           100    100\n  Bolt    1 minute           500    250\n\n  Weft won't let you save a cron with a schedule more frequent than your plan allows.\n  It will also disable a cron that produces more tags than your plan limit.\n\n  Disabled crons (see Runtime analysis (man crons)) don't count\n  against your plan limit.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOlder data and your history budget\x1b[0m\n  Recent data (the last 14 days) is always available. Reading older data counts\n  against a monthly history budget, in lines read:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mPlan    Older data read per month\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------  -------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Trial   none\n  Swatch  34 million lines\n  Yard    1.6 billion lines\n\n  cat /etc/usage shows how much of it you have used. When it is used up, a query\n  that needs older data fails with a message saying so and when the month resets;\n  queries over recent data are not affected.\n\n  A cron fires over a window as long as its schedule, so a cron that looks back\n  nearly 14 days can reach older data. Saving one warns you. If a fire needs older\n  data after the budget is used up, it fails, weft disables the cron, and a\n  notice goes to your system alias. To turn it back on, shorten the schedule or\n  wait for the budget to reset, then edit /cron.d/<name> and remove the\n  # enabled: false line.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRetention\x1b[0m\n  Raw results are rolled up into progressively coarser summaries as they age.\n  Every summary stores the count, sum, sum of squares, and minimum and maximum\n  per tag, so averages, minima, maxima and standard deviations stay exact when\n  buckets are merged.  Only runs with a numeric value are summarised; text-only\n  results exist in the raw history alone.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGranularity                                      Kept for\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------------------------------------------  --------\x1b[0m\n  Raw results (every run, full stdout and stderr)  7 days\n  Hourly summary                                   14 days\n  Four-hour summary                                35 days\n  Daily summary                                    400 days\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAnalyzing cron data\x1b[0m\n  The weft::* family of functions reads your raw cron history from inside a\n  script. They come in three flavors:\n\n  - Aggregates return the most recent value, an aggregate over a\n    window, or an anchor like the slope between two endpoints.\n  - Predicates return a boolean — \"is this cron above 50?\",\n    \"did it just change?\"\n  - Comparators turn a piped value into a boolean.\n\n  With the exception of some comparators, each function is available in both bash\n  and Lua.  Bash uses :: as the namespace separator, and Lua uses .. The\n  arguments are the same in both.\n\n  For composing predicates into composite alerts, see\n  Alerts (help alerts).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAggregates\x1b[0m\n  All aggregates take a cron name and (with the exception of weft::latest and\n  weft::previous) a duration window. Durations are a number and a unit: 30s,\n  5m, 1h, 6h, plus d for a day (24 hours) and w for a week (7 days).\n  Units combine (e.g. 1d12h). Some functions will truncate your duration to the\n  raw data window (7 days) because the math doesn't work on the compressed cron\n  results. For all others, the limiting factor is your plan's retention.\n\n  Depending on the context, weft will verify either statically or at runtime that\n  its first argument is a cron and returns a type that matches the signature of\n  the aggregate.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash            Lua             Accepts/Returns  Longest window  Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------  --------------  --------------  --------------  --------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::avg       weft.avg(cron,  number          400d            Mean of\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            numeric\n                                                                  results in\n                                                                  window\n  weft::count     weft.count(cron,  number          400d            Count of\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            numeric\n                                                                  results in\n                                                                  window\n  weft::delta     weft.delta(cron,  number          7d              latest −\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            first over\n                                                                  window\n  weft::first     weft.first(cron,  number          7d              Oldest numeric\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            value still in\n                                                                  window\n  weft::latest    weft.latest(cron)  text/number     n/a             Trimmed stdout\n  <cron>                                                          of the most\n                                                                  recent run\n  weft::max       weft.max(cron,  number          400d            Largest\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            numeric result\n                                                                  in window\n  weft::min       weft.min(cron,  number          400d            Smallest\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            numeric result\n                                                                  in window\n  weft::percentile  weft.percentile(cron,  number          7d              Linearly-interpolated\n  <cron> <dur>    dur, p)                                         p-th\n  <p>                                                             percentile, p\n                                                                  ∈ [0,100]\n  weft::previous  weft.previous(cron)  text/number     n/a             Stdout of the\n  <cron>                                                          second-most-recent\n                                                                  run (\"\" if\n                                                                  fewer than 2\n                                                                  exist)\n  weft::rate      weft.rate(cron,  number          400d            sum /\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            window_seconds\n  weft::slope     weft.slope(cron,  number          7d              delta /\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            window_seconds\n  weft::stddev    weft.stddev(cron,  number          400d            Population\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            stddev; needs\n                                                                  ≥2 results\n  weft::sum       weft.sum(cron,  number          400d            Sum of numeric\n  <cron> <dur>    dur)                                            results in\n                                                                  window\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mPredicates\x1b[0m\n  The following predicate functions are available:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash                      Lua                        True when\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------------  -------------------------  -------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::is_above <cron>     weft.is_above(cron, thr,   latest > thr (or min over\n  <thr> [<dur>              [dur, [avg]])              dur > thr, or avg > thr\n  [--average]]                                         with --average)\n  weft::is_below <cron>     weft.is_below(cron, thr,   latest < thr (or max over\n  <thr> [<dur>              [dur, [avg]])              dur < thr, or avg < thr\n  [--average]]                                         with --average)\n  weft::is_absent <cron>    weft.is_absent(cron,       the cron has no result:\n  [<dur>]                   [dur])                     it doesn't exist, has\n                                                       never run, or its latest\n                                                       output is empty (with\n                                                       dur: no result landed in\n                                                       the last dur). A bad or\n                                                       over-cap dur (more than\n                                                       7d) or an unreadable\n                                                       history is false; the\n                                                       bash form says why on\n                                                       stderr\n  weft::has_changed <cron>  weft.has_changed(cron)     latest ≠ previous tick\n  weft::is_rising <cron>    weft.is_rising(cron,       (latest −\n  [<pct> [<dur>]]           [pct, [dur]])              baseline)/baseline ≥\n                                                       pct% (baseline = prev\n                                                       tick, or window avg if\n                                                       dur given)\n  weft::is_falling <cron>   weft.is_falling(cron,      (baseline −\n  [<pct> [<dur>]]           [pct, [dur]])              latest)/baseline ≥ pct%\n  weft::is_outlier <cron>   weft.is_outlier(cron, n,   \\|latest − avg\\| >\n  <n> <dur>                 dur, [dir])                n·stddev over dur\n  [--above|--below]\n  weft::is_seasonal <cron>  weft.is_seasonal(cron, n,  \\|latest −\n  <n> <period> <dur>        period, dur)               same-time-of-period avg\\|\n                                                       > n·stddev\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTruthiness\x1b[0m\n  In bash a predicate reports its result as an exit code, and in Lua as a plain bool.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mSituation                         Exit code  Lua\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------  ---------  ---------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Condition holds                   0 (true)   true\n  Condition doesn't hold            1 (false)  false\n  Can't evaluate: the cron doesn't  1 (false)  false\n  exist, its output isn't numeric,\n  or there isn't enough history\n  yet\n  Bad arguments (a missing          2          error raised on bad argument\n  argument, an unparseable                     types, unparseable duration\n  threshold or duration)                       returns false\n\n  Because \"no\" and \"can't tell\" both exit 1, ! turns an unknown into true. Rather than negating, use the opposite predicate, and pair it with weft::is_absent to catch a cron that has stopped reporting.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAnomaly guards\x1b[0m\n  is_outlier and is_seasonal both gate against zero-stddev histories:\n  if the historical stddev drops below 0.01 · |avg|, the predicate\n  returns false. is_seasonal\n  additionally requires at least 4 same-time samples.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSeasonal alignment\x1b[0m\n  For is_seasonal, the period is the recurring interval and\n  window is how far back to sample. With period=1d window=4d, the\n  predicate compares the latest value to the four values captured at\n  this same hour on each of the last four days. Period alignment uses UTC.\n\n  Because the window is limited to the 7 days of raw results (see the table\n  above), a weekly period can never gather the 4 samples it needs, so\n  is_seasonal is only useful with periods of a day or less.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mStdin comparators\x1b[0m\n  Lua has comparison operators that everyone would expect, so you can write\n  weft.avg('error-rate', '5m') > 50 and everyone knows what you mean. The\n  unique beauty of bash adds a tasty bit of enigma:\n\n    [ \"$(weft::avg error-rate 5m)\" -gt 50 ]\n\n  And while we love bash's quirky conditionals, they do not compose nicely in\n  pipelines. Furthermore, bash comparisons only work on integers, whereas weft's\n  aggregate functions return floats. Rather than ask you to pipe to bc, we\n  provide a couple convenience functions that can handle numbers with dots in them:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mBash               True when\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------------  ---------------------------\x1b[0m\n  weft::is_gt <thr>  stdin parses as float > thr\n  weft::is_lt <thr>  stdin parses as float < thr\n\n  Thus, instead of the previous example, you can do:\n\n    weft::avg error-rate 5m | weft::is_gt 50\n\n  Both comparators treat empty or non-numeric input as false.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mStatic analysis\x1b[0m\n  Whenever you write a cron, weft analyzes its contents for a few types of correctness:\n\n  - Schedule -- a schedule line must be present, conform to the accepted\n    grammar (man crons), and fire at a frequency allowed by your\n    organization's plan\n  - Syntax -- the script must parse as syntactically valid bash (and awk,\n    sed, jq or Lua if using those interpreters)\n  - Aggregates -- beyond type signatures, weft:: aggregate functions read\n    stored history and ignore their input, so if they follow a pipe, you're doing\n    something wrong\n  - Execution limits -- tail -f is disallowed, since a script has to run to\n    completion, as is range, since the range of a cron is determined by its\n    schedule\n  - Output schema -- # tag: cannot be set without # value:\n  - Types -- weft will enforce correct typing of cron arguments to aggregate\n    and predicate functions\n\n  If you run afoul of any of these rules, weft will refuse to write the contents\n  to /cron.d (or /bin).\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRuntime analysis\x1b[0m\n  Weft also watches how your crons behave once they are running. Each run has a\n  budget of 2 seconds. A run that exceeds it is cancelled and recorded with the\n  status timeout, and no further output from that run is saved. Three timeouts\n  in a row disable the cron; a run that finishes in time resets the count.\n\n  A notification target that doesn't accept an alert within 10 seconds counts\n  toward those three as well (see Key behaviors (help alerts)).\n\n  Each run's output is evaluated against its type\n  declaration (man crons). A run whose output breaks the rules\n  is recorded with the status invalid and the reason in its stderr, and is\n  disabled.\n\n  A type mismatch between aggregate or predicate functions and their cron\n  argument is normally checked statically, but in cases where the mismatch is\n  known only at runtime by passing a variable, the  cron will be disabled then.\n\n  As described above, type declarations are verified and enforced, including JSON\n  extraction. Empty output is treated as conformant.\n\n  Type/parsing related runtime failures result in the cron being disabled\n  immediately.  A disabled cron stays in /cron.d with a marker at the top of\n  its file:\n\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    # enabled: false\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l\n\n  You can also write # enabled: false yourself to pause a cron. To turn it back\n  on, save the file without that line (or with # enabled: true). Re-enabling\n  starts the count over, and is refused if you are already at your plan's cron\n  limit.\n"

// ManualDashboards — Dashboards (generated from docs/manual/dashboards.md).
const ManualDashboards = "DASHBOARDS(1)                   Weft Commands                    DASHBOARDS(1)\n\n  Weft allows you to visualize the results of your crons in the terminal and in\n  the browser, both as ad hoc charts as you are in the middle of an investigation\n  and as durable dashboards you come back to routinely.\n\n  Since dashboards are built on some of the same grammar as charts, let's start\n  with the chart command.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe chart command\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSynopsis\x1b[0m\n    chart <cron>       [-a <fn>] [--bucket <dur>] [--kind KIND] [--title <text>]\n                       [-f|--follow [-i INTERVAL]]\n    <pipeline> | chart [-x <field>] [-y <field>] [-a <fn>] [-t <field>]\n                       [--bucket <dur>] [--kind KIND] [--title <text>]\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mHow chart works\x1b[0m\n  chart has two forms:\n\n  Standalone: chart <cron> draws what a cron has\n  stored (man crons) over the current range. A tagged cron\n  draws one line per tag for the top eight series, with the rest collapsed into a\n  single line.  With --follow, the chart redraws every INTERVAL until you\n  exit. This requires the current range to be relative.\n\n  End of pipeline: <pipeline> | chart [...] draws the pipeline's output\n  once and returns to the prompt. chart must be the last stage; piping its\n  output onward is an error. By default, the x axis unit is line number of the\n  input, and the y axis values are the first whitespace-separated field of each\n  line. Both can be overridden with flags.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mchart options\x1b[0m\n  - -x <field>[::time[(<format>)]]: <field> is either a 1-indexed whitespace\n    column (-x 1) or a .-prefixed JSON path (-x .ts). Derived automatically\n    for crons. Value parse as an integer unless cast:\n      - ::time: x is a time in RFC3339 or epoch seconds (-x 1::time).\n      - ::time(<format>): x is a time in a strftime format A row that doesn't\n        match the supplied format aborts the chart with an error.\n      - You can supply a column range <start>-<end> when casting to time for\n        stamps that span two fields: -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)'.\n\n  - -y <field>:  same grammar as -x, except does not support time casts. Derived\n    automatically for crons\n  - -a, --aggregate <fn>: combines y values with the same x value with\n    count, sum, avg, min, max, p50, p95, identity.  Defaults to\n    identity, or count when --bucket is set.\n  - -t, --tag <field>: draw one line per distinct value of <field>, such\n    as one per host. Same field grammar as -x and -y. Only valid with --kind\n    line (the default when --tag is set). Each line gets its own color and a\n    legend row.\n  - --bucket <DURATION>: group rows into time buckets on x. Arguments formatted\n    as \\d[.\\d](ms|s|m|h).\n  - --kind <line|sparkline|bar>: chart kind. Default bar, or line when\n    --bucket or --tag is set.\n  - --title <text>: chart title.\n\n  -x, -y and -t must all be columns or all be JSON paths. -t\n  needs one JSON object per line, so use jq -c.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mchart examples\x1b[0m\n    # One bar per line\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq '.memory.pct' | chart\n\n    # Error lines per five minutes. The stamp \"2026/10/13 15:04:05\" spans\n    # columns 1 and 2.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR \\\n      | chart -x '1-2::time(%Y/%m/%d %H:%M:%S)' --bucket 5m\n\n    # 95th-percentile memory use per minute from JSON fields\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --bucket 1m -a p95\n\n    # One bar per host, average memory use\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log | jq -c '{host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .host -y .pct -a avg\n\n    # One line per host over time.\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/host.log \\\n      | jq -c '{ts: $ts, host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | chart -x .ts -y .pct --tag .host --bucket 1m -a avg\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mBuilding a dashboard\x1b[0m\n  Dashboards are rendered from a YAML specification in /etc/dashboards. The\n  YAML arranges your crons into sections of tiles. Tiles are a superset of what\n  chart renders, with keys mapped to the flags and arguments provided to that\n  command. A brief example:\n\n    # written to /etc/dashboards/prod.yaml\n    title: Production\n    tiles:\n      - kind: sparkline\n        cron: error-rate\n        title: Errors per 5 minutes\n\n  You can open it in the terminal with the dashboard command. It will also\n  appear on the Dashboards page of the web app. You can have as many\n  dashboards as you like, each in its own file.  ## The dashboard command\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mdashboard synopsis\x1b[0m\n    dashboard\n    dashboard <name>\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDashboard YAML\x1b[0m\n  The dashboard YAML schema has lets you have one flat bucket for your tiles. You can also group your tiles into sections. The top-level keys in the schema are as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField     Required  Notes\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------  --------  ----------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  title     no        Heading at the top of the dashboard\n  sections  one of    Array of sections, each with an optional title and a\n                      required tiles list\n  tiles     one of    A flat tile list (one untitled section)\n\n  A simple example dashboard with sections:\n\n    title: Production\n    sections:\n      - title: Errors\n        tiles:\n          - kind: sparkline\n            cron: error-rate\n            size: md\n      - title: Capacity\n        tiles:\n          - kind: counter\n            cron: pending-jobs\n            format: \"%d jobs\"\n            size: sm\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTile kinds\x1b[0m\n  Dashboard tiles are built on the same foundation as the chart command, so you can easily convert a 1-liner to to the appropriate YAML. For example,chart error-rate --kind bar -a max --bucket 1h --title \"Worst hour\" becomes:\n\n    - kind: bar\n      cron: error-rate\n      aggregate: max\n      bucket: 1h\n      title: Worst hour\n\n  In addition to the chart kinds (line, sparkline, and bar), dashboards\n  provide a few more options for laying out values on a tile grid where, in the\n  terminal, normal output works just fine:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKind     Default display                    Extra options\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ---------------------------------  ----------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  counter  latest value (on a tagged cron,    format -- strftime e.g. \"%d ms\",\n           the highest)                       `\"%.1f%%\"\n  gauge    latest value as a bar from 0 to    max\n           max (default 100)\n  table    latest run, row per tag            -\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRendering alerts\x1b[0m\n  If a tile targets a cron that uses alert, weft  will render that alert level\n  automatically as a part of the tile wherever possible:\n\n  - Lines, sparklines, and bar charts draw the cutoff as a red line\n  - Counters and gauges shows their content in red when the value exceeds the threshold\n  - Tables highlight offending rows in red\n\n  A counter, gauge or table that aggregates over a range compares the aggregate,\n  not the latest run. Other alert conditions, such as rising or absent, have\n  no single number to draw. To change where a tile turns red, change the alert.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mLayout\x1b[0m\n  Tiles appear in YAML order, flowing left to right and then down. You can\n  specify width of a given tile with the optional size key. Valid values are\n  sm, md, and lg. lg will always be full width, with packing of sm and\n  md tiles best effort in both browser and terminal.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mBuilding dashboards with robots\x1b[0m\n  The weft MCP server provides tools for proposing dashboards (and crons if you\n  are starting from scratch). Worth trying if you need a little help getting\n  started.\n"

// ManualFastPipelines — How the weft shell works (generated from docs/manual/fast-pipelines.md).
const ManualFastPipelines = "FAST-PIPELINES(1)               Weft Commands                FAST-PIPELINES(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mHow the weft shell works\x1b[0m\n  Weft stores your logs in a well-indexed distributed log store. Your current\n  range (man range) is in your PS1 for a reason -- this store is\n  time-sharded and the retrieval mechanism can eliminate whole storage nodes\n  based on that range value.\n\n  But for a larger timeframe for a chatty service, this is not enough. To make\n  your log spelunking speedy, the weft shell will:\n\n  - watch for 1-liners that read from log files and trigger a static analysis\n    process\n  - walk the pipeline from left to right, pushing parts of AWK and jq programs,\n    plus many standard bash operations, into an intermediate representation\n  - recognize when it hits a pipeline stage whose semantics cannot be pushed into\n    a single pass on the log store\n  - compile the IR into a two-part plan: optimized retrieval (a log store\n    query), and post-retrieval (the remainders of the pipeline that must be\n    executed in the interpreter)\n  - deliver that plan to the executor, which then sends rows back to your shell\n\n  As the name implies, the more of your pipeline that makes it into optimized\n  retrieval, the sooner you get your log lines. The types of optimization can be\n  grouped roughly into line selection, field selection and aggregation.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOptimized line selection\x1b[0m\n  - grep [-ivE]\n  - sed -n '/PATTERN/p', sed '/PATTERN/d', and their negated forms\n  - head [-n] and tail [-n]\n  - Most AWK patterns, like awk '$4 ~ /foo/'\n  - jq select function calls, i.e. jq 'select(.bar == \"baz\")'\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOptimized field selection\x1b[0m\n  - AWK actions with field variables (awk '{print $2, $7}') -- $NF also gets optimized\n  - cut -d DELIM -f LIST, where LIST is discrete fields or a trailing open range (i.e. 3-)\n  - jq property remapping like jq '{status, responseMs}\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mOptimized aggregation\x1b[0m\n  - grep -c\n  - wc [-cl]\n  - sort [-urn] and uniq [-c]\n  - the standard AWK bucketed count pattern (awk '{ c[$1]++ } END { for (k in c) print k, c[k] }')\n\n  Depending on whether you have structured logs in JSON or good old fashioned\n  unstructured lines, the best way to optimize is to lean on jq or AWK, and\n  wherever possible, to use metavariables (man metavars) in your AWK patterns or\n  jq select calls.\n\n  Other commands, or these in the wrong order, is going to execute\n  post-retrieval. If your 1-liners are timing out, you might want to run\n  explain for hints of what could move into optimized retrieval.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mexplain\x1b[0m\n  explain is a builtin function that will show you which stages of your\n  pipeline run optimized, partly optimized, or post-retrieval. Sometimes it can\n  provide you with helpful hints for rearranging things to speed it up.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mExamples\x1b[0m\n  This one's going to be very fast, nothing to improve:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' | grep timeout | head -20\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  The next one's only partly optimized, but an easy fix. The awk pattern is\n  optimized, but printf makes the action more than the compiler can push into\n  the store, so the stage as a whole is only partly optimized:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { printf \"[%s] %s\\n\", HOST, $2 }'\n      1  awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { printf \"[%s] %s\\n\", HOST, $2 }'  partly optimized\n           pattern: optimized; action: post-retrieval\n           Action does more than print the line or its fields.\n           Try: Print only, e.g. `print $1, HOST`.\n\n    Barrier: stage 1.\n\n  If we don't need that formatting, we can get that whole stage optimized by following the hint:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { print HOST, $2 }'\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  But if we really want the formatting, it's still valuable to move it into a\n  second stage so that the optimized stages aren't returning whole rows:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { print HOST, $2 }' | awk '{ printf \"[%s] %s\\n\", $1, $2 }'\n      1  awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { print HOST, $2 }'  optimized retrieval\n      2  awk '{ printf \"[%s] %s\\n\", $1, $2 }'       post-retrieval\n           Action does more than print the line or its fields.\n           Try: Print only, e.g. `print $1, HOST`.\n\n    Barrier: stage 2.\n\n  jq behaves the same way. Its leading selects are optimized and picking\n  fields afterwards is too, but building a string (\"\\(.path) \\(.ms)\") or\n  adding to a value (.path + \"?\") leaves the rest post-retrieval.\n\n  If you have commands that stop optimization before stages that otherwise could\n  be optimized, weft won't figure it out. For example, messing with string casing\n  before a line count.\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep timeout | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { print toupper($0) }' | wc -l\n      1  grep timeout                                  optimized retrieval\n      2  awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\" { print toupper($0) }'  partly optimized\n           pattern: optimized; action: post-retrieval\n           Action does more than print the line or its fields.\n           Try: Print only, e.g. `print $1, HOST`.\n      3  wc -l                                         post-retrieval\n\n    Barrier: stage 2.\n\n  This is perhaps a contrived example, but if you work in the shell by\n  iteratively glomming more stages onto a pipeline, you very well might just\n  forget that you no longer need some part of it:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep timeout | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' | wc -l\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  A single unsupported flag on the first stage drags the whole pipeline into\n  post-retrieval:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | grep -E 'timeout|refused' | grep -v health | wc -l\n      1  grep -E 'timeout|refused'  post-retrieval\n           grep optimized with -i, -v, or no flags; not -E.\n      2  grep -v health             post-retrieval\n      3  wc -l                      post-retrieval\n\n    Barrier: stage 1.\n\n  An awk pattern matches the same lines with the same regular expression, and\n  it is optimized, so swapping just the first stage fixes all three:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | awk '/timeout|refused/' | grep -v health | wc -l\n    Every stage: optimized retrieval.\n\n  Some work really does need every row. This one rewrites each line to group\n  messages that differ only in their numbers, which can only be done in\n  post-retrieval computation:\n\n    $ explain cat /log/acme/api/main.log | sed 's/[0-9][0-9]*/N/g' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n      1  sed 's/[0-9][0-9]*/N/g'  post-retrieval\n           sed optimized only as `sed -n '/re/p'`.\n           Try: Matching lines: use grep. Substitution: post-retrieval, so put it after narrowing stages.\n      2  sort                     post-retrieval\n      3  uniq -c                  post-retrieval\n      4  sort -rn                 post-retrieval\n           No optimized form.\n      5  head                     post-retrieval\n\n    Barrier: stage 1.\n\n  No rewrite of this pipeline keeps its meaning and gets faster. What you can do\n  is send fewer rows into it: a smaller range (man range), a\n  narrower source, or a grep ahead of the sed for the lines you care about.\n"

// ManFilesystem — File system (generated from docs/manual/filesystem.md).
const ManFilesystem = "\x1b[38;5;208mThe weft file system\x1b[0m\n  The weft shell provides a synthetic file system for organizing and navigating\n  your logs, scripts, notes, and configuration. To whatever extent possible,\n  we've tried to make this feel like real Unix, so you don't have to learn new\n  things. Given that it's not exactly real, an overrview is warranted.\n\n  The shell exposes four mount points, detailed below.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mMount        Purpose                                  Writeable by  Versioning\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------  ---------------------------------------  ------------  ----------\x1b[0m\n  /log         Your logs, with a directory/file layout  nobody        no\n               depdented on your sources (man\n               overview)\n  /tmp         Scratch space for your investigations    whole org     no\n               staging results\n  /bin         Reusable scripts in bash or Lua          binmgr        yes\n  /cron.d      Crons (man crons): pipelines that run    cronmgr       yes\n               on a schedule.\n  /etc         Dashboard (man dashboards) and           configmgr     yes\n               Notification (help alerts)\n               configuration\n  /etc/passwd  teams (man teams) roster                 nobody        no\n  /etc/usage   plan usage and metering                  nobody        no\n\n  With the exception of tmp, all writeable destinations will validate their\n  contents and prevent writes if validation fails. Otherwise, automatic\n  versioning applies.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208m/tmp\x1b[0m\n  This is your staging area for results of grepping and awking and awking your\n  logs. Without this, commands like comm, join, and diff aren't really\n  useful. So dump some filtered results in here like so:\n\n    cat /log/myco/api/main.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n\n  You now have something a bit easier to poke at that the firehose of stdout your\n  application is spewing.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLimitations\x1b[0m\n  - TTL. We know your homedir hygiene is terrible, so /tmp files are\n    deleted 48 hours after its last write. >, >>, cp, mv and touch\n    reset the clock; ls -l shows the mtime. If you want to accumulate longer-term\n    results, use crons (man crons) instead.\n  - Bytes. All tmp files are limited to 512 KB. Total tmp storage limits depend on your org's plan.\n  - +rw. Files in /tmp are not executable. If you want to run it, put it in /bin.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCustom scripts in /bin\x1b[0m\n  Anything you can type at the shell prompt can be saved as a named script under\n  /bin/. /bin is automatically on your path for shell sessions and crons.\n  Create scripts with vi or with cat - > myscript.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLanguage support\x1b[0m\n  The weft shell supports a subset of bash and Lua.\n  Scripts default to bash. If you're slinging Lua, add a .lua or a shebang\n\n    #!/usr/bin/env lua\n    -- /bin/report\n    local name = arg[1]\n    local avg = weft.avg(name, \"1h\")\n    print(string.format(\"%s averaged %.2f over the last hour\", name, avg))\n\n  Weft supports Lua 5.1, including the standard table, string,\n  math, and coroutine libraries, plus a restricted os (os.clock,\n  os.date, os.time, os.difftime).\n\n  On top of the standard library, weft adds:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGlobal                           Purpose\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  arg                              Positional arguments — arg[1], arg[2], …\n  print(...)                       Write to stdout (tab-separated args, newline)\n  eprint(...)                      Same shape, writes to stderr\n  io.write(...)                    Stdout without trailing newline\n  io.read(\"*l\" | \"*a\" | \"*n\")      Read a line, all of stdin, or a number\n  lines()                          Iterator over stdin lines — for line in\n                                   lines() do ... end\n  json.encode(v) / json.decode(s)  JSON ↔ Lua table\n  weft.*                           aggregate and predicate functions\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mVersion history\x1b[0m\n  Every save to a script in /bin, a cron in /cron.d, a dashboard in\n  /etc/dashboards, or /etc/notify.yaml is captured in a short, browseable\n  history. You access that history and manipulate it with the git command.\n\n  Weft git is a very limited subset of true git behavior and is thus named\n  simply to support your muscle memory. Supported commands (git log, git\n  show, git diff, git blame git restore) behave as you'd expect, but\n  there's a lot not there by design. git --help lists what is supported.\n  Unsupported commands and unsupported flags are refused.\n\n  Committing is automatic and across your entire org's file structure. There is\n  no branching, no staging area, and no remotes. Therefore there are no merge or\n  rebase commands either.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLimits\x1b[0m\n  Weft keeps each file's latest six versions (the live one plus the\n  five before it). Older versions are deleted.\n"

// ManualHeroku — Heroku (generated from docs/manual/heroku.md).
const ManualHeroku = "HEROKU(1)                       Weft Commands                        HEROKU(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnecting a Heroku app\x1b[0m\n  Weft accepts logs directly from Heroku's HTTPS log-drain protocol. This page\n  covers the Heroku-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log\n  source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces).  There's nothing special about Heroku\n  that requires its own token, but it's probably tidier if it's got its own.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Add the drain\x1b[0m\n  For each Heroku app you want to ingest, run heroku drains:add with the\n  token embedded in the weft drain URL. Your token goes in the URL like so:\n\n    heroku drains:add \"https://api.w3ft.net/api/ingest/heroku/wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\" -a myapp\n    heroku drains:add \"https://api.w3ft.net/api/ingest/heroku/wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\" -a sister-app\n\n  Heroku starts delivering within a few seconds. Then confirm rows are landing\n  and, if you want nice, speedy lookups, approve a\n  schema (man overview). Heroku schemas are per\n  service, so it covers the web, cron and build files under it.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mHow dynos and procs map to log files\x1b[0m\n  The Heroku app name is the weft service, and the process type from the\n  procid (web, worker, router) is the log kind.\n  (See Namespaces for how every producer chooses its service name.)\n  The full procid (web.1, web.2) is stamped on instance, so cross-dyno\n  aggregation stays cheap and a single tail /log/.../web.log covers every web\n  dyno at once.\n\n  Weft maps Heroku sources to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mHeroku source              weft file                  metavariables\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------------------  -------------------------  ------------------------\x1b[0m\n  myapp web dyno web.1       /log/<ns>/myapp/web.log    instance=web.1\n  myapp worker dyno          /log/<ns>/myapp/worker.log  instance=worker.1\n  worker.1\n  myapp router               /log/<ns>/myapp/router.log  instance=router\n  myapp heroku-postgres      /log/<ns>/myapp/heroku-postgres.log  instance=heroku-postgres\n  Platform events (deploys,  /log/<ns>/myapp/platform.log\n  restarts, scales)\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetric lines auto-route to metrics.log\x1b[0m\n  Heroku's runtime-heroku-metrics labs feature (and most add-ons) emit\n  l2met-shaped lines like:\n\n    source=web.1 sample#memory_total=512.00MB sample#memory_rss=487.21MB sample#load_avg_1m=0.42\n\n  Weft detects these and routes them to <appname>/metrics.log, with the\n  key/value pairs converted to JSON (numeric where the value is\n  numeric, with trailing units like MB kept on a sibling\n  <key>_unit field). The original line also flows into the dyno's\n  log file, so grep memory_total /log/.../web.log matches it.\n\n    # Enable the metric stream on a Heroku app (one-time)\n    heroku labs:enable \"runtime-heroku-metrics\" -a myapp\n\n    # Then read it like any other JSON log source\n    weft sh\n    $ tail /log/myco/prod/myapp/metrics.log | jq 'select(.memory_total > 400)'\n"

// ManualMetavars — Meta-variables (generated from docs/manual/metavars.md).
const ManualMetavars = "METAVARS(1)                     Weft Commands                      METAVARS(1)\n\n  Each stored row carries a fixed set of indexed columns beside its text and/or JSON\n  payload. They are exposed to AWK and jq under the variable names below.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mawk                    jq                       Source\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------------------  -----------------------  ------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  NAMESPACE              $namespace               the <namespace> path segment /\n                                                  agent token's namespace\n  SERVICE                $service                 source declaration, log\n                                                  filename, or HTTP ingest\n                                                  payload\n  KIND                   $kind                    the stream kind (main,\n                                                  heartbeats, metrics, runtime)\n  HOST (alias INSTANCE)  $host (alias $instance)  the agent's hostname\n  LEVEL                  $level                   schema-extracted from the row\n  TS                     $ts                      schema-extracted from the row\n  DEPLOYMENT             $deployment              weftd deployment: config (or\n                                                  k8s/ASG)\n  ENV                    $env                     bound to the agent token at\n                                                  mint time\n  VERSION                $version                 weftd metavariables.version\n  REGION                 $region                  cloud metadata probe at agent\n                                                  startup\n  NODE                   $node                    weftd metavariables.node\n  TRACE_ID               $trace_id                the SDK's trace ID, or\n                                                  schema-extracted from the row\n  SPAN_NAME              $span_name               on a span's lines, from the\n                                                  SDK header or the schema\n  SPAN_ID                $span_id                 on a span's lines, from the\n                                                  SDK header or the schema\n  PARENT_SPAN_ID         $parent_span_id          on a span's lines, the span\n                                                  that started it\n  DURATION_NS            $duration_ns             on the line that ends a span,\n                                                  its duration in nanoseconds\n  EVENT_ID               $event_id                shared by the lines of one\n                                                  multi-line event\n  LINE_NO                $line_no                 the line's 0-based position\n                                                  within its event\n"

// ManualNamespaces — Namespaces & tokens (generated from docs/manual/namespaces.md).
const ManualNamespaces = "NAMESPACES(1)                   Weft Commands                    NAMESPACES(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mNamespaces\x1b[0m\n  Namespaces group related log sources. We usually think if namespaces as buckets\n  for a single application, but it's your bucket, so do what you want. Namespaces\n  are as granular as permission grants get, so keep that in mind when laying out\n  your log sources.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCreating a namespace\x1b[0m\n  To create a new namespace in your browser, go to Namespaces in the sidebar\n  and click new. Choose something easy to type for the name — it'll be in\n  the path of every log file you configure under it.\n\n  Or, from the CLI:\n\n    weft namespace create babbage\n\n  To remove one, weft namespace remove babbage. It refuses while the namespace\n  still has sources or live tokens, and lists them; --force removes the\n  namespace and everything in it. Logs already stored age out under normal\n  retention.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTokens\x1b[0m\n  Weft uses a simple token-based authentication for all log producers, whether\n  that be weftd, Render, Heroku, or Kinesis.  Each token is scoped to exactly one\n  namespace, but you can have as many as you like within a namespace. In fact, we\n  encourage it! You can optionally tag your tokens with an environment name,\n  allowing you to keep a staging, sandbox, and prod environment under the same\n  namespace but keep their logs separate.\n\n  You can always view your amassed sea of tokens under each namespace in the\n  browser, or in your terminal with weft token list.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCreating a token\x1b[0m\n  To create a token in the browser, go to Namespaces, select the namespace\n  you want the token to be in, and click new token. You can optionally bind a\n  environment tag (prod, staging, qa, etc.) and a label. What's the label\n  for? You tell me! It's nice to have options. The environment will have log file\n  path significance, whereas the label does not (but you can still filter on it).\n\n  From the CLI:\n\n    weft token create babbage --label api-prod --env prod\n\n  The token is only shown once regardless of where you create it, so put it\n  somewhere safe! If you lose it, revoke it and create a new one -- either by\n  clicking \"revoke\" in the browser or with weft token revoke --id <id> on the\n  command line.\n"

// ManualIntroduction — Introduction (generated from docs/manual/query-logs.md).
const ManualIntroduction = "INTRODUCTION(1)                 Weft Commands                  INTRODUCTION(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mThe weft shell\x1b[0m\n  The goal of weft is, in a nutshell, to enable efficient querying of distributed\n  log storage using the time-tested tools of Unix. As such, we've made an effort\n  to provide dopplegangers of enough of coreutils that you can mostly forget you\n  aren't just ssh'ed into your servers directly. Stitch together pipelines to\n  your heart's content. See Builtins (help builtins) for a complete list of\n  supported Unix commands and interpreters.\n\n  Some 1-liners are easier for our tools to translate into log queries than\n  others, and we've invested the most effort into AWK, sed, grep, and jq. Leaning\n  on these will result in the most efficient retrieval of rows from the log\n  store. We're working on exposing something like optimization hints in a coming\n  release.\n\n  Again, the principle of least surprise is our north star here. Hopefully you\n  never know you aren't on an old-school log server. And hopefully when you run\n  into that surprise, we've got nice clear error messages. But like us, weft is a\n  work in progress.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mKey differences\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSandboxness\x1b[0m\n  Weft is a log shell, and although it allows a certain amount of scripting for those purposes, it is not an operating system, a general purpose programming environment, or a VPS you control. There may be things you would like to do that you cannot. The veneer of Unix is only as thick as we needed it to be.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mRange\x1b[0m\n  The timerange of logs you are working with is displayed in your PS1. How far back in time you can set it depends on your plan. Spoiler, if you ship a lot of logs and you wanna query 3 months, it's gonna be slower. You can change it with the range command.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetavariables\x1b[0m\n  We inject some preset variables into invocations of AWK and jq that are helpful in narrowing down the lines you need; these are all indexed nicely so including them in an AWK pattern or a jq select() should get you your log lines faster. See the full reference (man metavars) for more details.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mPaths\x1b[0m\n  Depending on what mechanism you are using to ship your logs to weft, the files\n  where those lines end up are going to look something like this:\n\n    /log/<namespace>/<service>/<kind>.log          3 segments: all envs\n    /log/<namespace>/<env>/<service>/<kind>.log    4 segments: one env\n\n  <kind> is the stream: main for ordinary logs, plus heartbeats,\n  metrics and runtime for services that emit telemetry. Which form a\n  service has depends on whether its producer token was minted with --env;\n  ls /log/myco shows env directories next to env-less services.\n\n  - cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log reads one env.\n  - cat /log/myco/api/main.log reads that service across every env.\n  - cat myco.log reads the whole namespace; filter with ENV == \"prod\".\n  - ls /log/myco/api lists a service's kinds.\n  - Globs only pick kinds inside one service: /log/myco/prod/api/*.log.\n    Globs that cross services or namespaces (cat *.log,\n    /log/myco/*/api/main.log) are refused.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mSome Examples\x1b[0m\n    # Tail logs in real time\n    tail -f myapp.log\n\n    # Errors in the default range (narrow it with `range 1h..`)\n    cat myapp.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"'\n\n    # Requests by status code\n    cat nginx.log | jq '.status' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n\n    # Everything in a namespace mentioning a failure\n    cat myco.log | grep \"connection refused\"\n\n    # Most recent 100 lines (cheap)\n    tail -n 100 myapp.log\n\n    # Production errors for one deployment, JSON form\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log \\\n      | jq 'select($level == \"ERROR\" and $deployment == \"api-east\")'\n\n    # Keep a result set and slice it several ways\n    cat api.log | awk 'LEVEL == \"ERROR\"' > /tmp/errs\n    grep timeout /tmp/errs | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n"

// ManualConnectRender — Render (generated from docs/manual/render.md).
const ManualConnectRender = "CONNECT-RENDER(1)               Weft Commands                CONNECT-RENDER(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a Render service\x1b[0m\n  Weft accepts logs directly from Render's built-in Log Streams feature using\n  syslog over TLS. This page covers the Render-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces). There's nothing special about Render\n  that requires its own token, but it's probably tidier if it's got its own.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Add the log stream in Render\x1b[0m\n  In the Render dashboard, click Integrations → Observability in the sidebar.\n  Scroll down to Log Streams, add a stream, and configure a generic/custom\n  syslog destination. Paste in the token from Step 1, along with the endpoint\n  (render.syslog.w3ft.net:6514).\n\n  Render starts streaming within a few seconds. If you want nice, speedy lookups,\n  approve a schema (man overview). Render\n  schemas are per service, so it covers the web, cron and build files under it.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding your logs\x1b[0m\n  Weft maps Render services to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mRender source                     weft file                     metavariables\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------  ----------------------------  --------------\x1b[0m\n  myservice web instance web-9nk6q  /log/<ns>/myservice/web.log   instance=9nk6q\n  myservice cron job cron-stqq5     /log/<ns>/myservice/cron.log  instance=stqq5\n  myservice build output bld-4fk2b  /log/<ns>/myservice/bld.log   instance=4fk2b\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mRender metrics\x1b[0m\n  Render's Metrics Streams feature pushes your services' system metrics to a\n  destination you choose. Weft accepts it over OpenTelemetry (OTLP/HTTP with a\n  JSON body) and keeps the system metrics: CPU, memory, network and disk. Metrics\n  Streams needs a Pro workspace or higher, and Render sends nothing for static\n  sites, cron jobs or one-off jobs.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mAdd the metrics stream in Render\x1b[0m\n  Use a token (man namespaces); the one you made for logs works.\n  In the Render dashboard, click Integrations → Observability, scroll to\n  Metrics Stream, and click + Add destination. Choose Custom and enter:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mField     Value\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------  ------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  Endpoint  https://api.w3ft.net/api/ingest/otlp\n  API key   your weft token\n\n  Render adds /v1/metrics to the endpoint itself, so leave the path off. Samples\n  arrive every 15 seconds per instance.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetrics land in host.log, network.log and disk.log\x1b[0m\n  Metrics go under the same service as that service's logs, as the kinds\n  host, network and disk. A compute instance has the same $host in its\n  metrics as in its logs, and the service's Render project is its $cluster.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mRender service            weft file                  fields\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------------  -------------------------  -------------------------\x1b[0m\n  myservice, CPU            /log/<ns>/myservice/host.log  cpu.cores, cpu.time_s\n  myservice, memory         /log/<ns>/myservice/host.log  memory.used_mb, total_mb,\n                                                       pct, rss_mb, cache_mb\n  myservice, network        /log/<ns>/myservice/network.log  rx_bytes_total,\n                                                       tx_bytes_total\n  myservice, persistent     /log/<ns>/myservice/disk.log  used_gb, total_gb\n  disk\n\n  Render sends each metric on its own schedule, so one sample is spread over\n  several rows and a row only holds the fields that arrived together. A field a\n  row doesn't have reads as null, so filter for the one you want:\n\n    weft sh\n    $ tail /log/myco/myservice/host.log | jq 'select(.memory.pct) | {host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}'\n\n  cpu.cores is the instance's CPU allowance (0.5 on a 512 MB instance, for\n  example). Memory pct is the share of the instance's memory limit. A disk row\n  has either used_gb or total_gb, and no mount path: Render reports a disk\n  under its own id, which is that row's $host.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mCPU time and network totals are running counters\x1b[0m\n  cpu.time_s, rx_bytes_total and tx_bytes_total are cumulative, counting\n  from when the instance started, like Render's own metrics. They are stored as\n  Render sends them, so to see CPU use or traffic over time you work out the\n  change between samples. A cron can do it per instance. This one reports each\n  instance's CPU as a percentage of its allowance, over the cron's window:\n\n    # /cron.d/render-cpu\n    # schedule: */5 * * * *\n    cat /log/myco/myservice/host.log \\\n      | jq -r 'select(.cpu.time_s) | \"\\($ts | sub(\" \";\"T\") + \"Z\" | fromdate) \\($host) \\(.cpu.time_s) \\(.cpu.cores)\"' \\\n      | awk '\n          {\n            t = $1; h = $2; c = $3; cores[h] = $4\n            if (h in prevc) {\n              d = c - prevc[h]\n              if (d < 0) d = c              # the instance restarted\n              used[h] += d\n              span[h] += t - prevt[h]\n            }\n            prevc[h] = c; prevt[h] = t\n          }\n          END {\n            for (h in used)\n              if (span[h] > 0 && cores[h] > 0)\n                printf \"%.2f %s\\n\", used[h] / span[h] / cores[h] * 100, h\n          }' \\\n      | alert above 90 -v 1 -t 2 --notify=ops\n\n  Each point is tagged with its instance, so you get one alert per instance, and\n  a chart of the cron draws one line per instance. Memory needs none of this,\n  since it isn't a counter:\n\n    cat /log/myco/myservice/host.log \\\n      | jq -c 'select(.memory.pct) | {host: $host, pct: .memory.pct}' \\\n      | alert above 90 -v .pct -t .host --notify=ops\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mWhat weft keeps\x1b[0m\n  Weft keeps CPU, memory, network and disk usage. The rest of what Render's\n  stream carries is dropped when it arrives, without an error: HTTP request\n  counts and latency, and the Postgres and Key Value internals (connections,\n  checkpoints, replication lag and so on).\n"

// ManualTeams — Teams & permissions (generated from docs/manual/team.md).
const ManualTeams = "TEAMS(1)                        Weft Commands                         TEAMS(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208m/etc/passwd\x1b[0m\n  Your org's members and what they can do is reflected on the accounts page, as\n  well as in the weft shell in /etc/passwd:\n\n    $ cat /etc/passwd\n    USER             ROLE     STATUS    GROUPS\n    alice@acme.com   admin    active    binmgr  cronmgr  configmgr  nsmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    bob@acme.com     member   active    binmgr  cronmgr  payments  payments+tokens\n    carol@acme.com   member   invited   —\n\n  Users are added or removed in the shell adduser|deluser <email address>, or\n  in the browser, account -> team. In either case, an invite will be\n  emailed. You must have the admin role to add or remove users.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGroups\x1b[0m\n  There are two main types of group:\n\n  Manager groups gate org-wide directories and namespace management:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGroup      Grants\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  -------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  binmgr     edit /bin (functions)\n  cronmgr    edit /cron.d (crons)\n  configmgr  edit /etc (notify.yaml, dashboards/\\*.yaml)\n  nsmgr      create and manage namespaces\n\n  Namespace groups control log access and token creation:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mGroup               Grants\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------  -----------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  <namespace>         read /log/<namespace>\n  <namespace>+tokens  create producer tokens for that namespace\n\n  A member can have either or both.\n\n  Admins hold groups as other members do, and the roster lists the groups they have.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mGranting and revoking\x1b[0m\n  Once someone is a member, add or drop their groups:\n\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments         # read payments logs\n    adduser dana@acme.com payments+tokens  # ...and mint producer tokens\n    adduser dana@acme.com cronmgr          # ...and edit /cron.d\n    deluser dana@acme.com payments         # revoke read\n\n  There is no clicky version of this -- you must use the shell. Same for getting the deets on what groups a user belongs to:\n\n    groups dana@acme.com                   # what dana can do\n    id dana@acme.com                       # role + groups\n\n  (groups and id with no argument describe you.)\n"

// ManTrace — Tracing (generated from docs/manual/trace.md).
const ManTrace = "TRACE(1)                        Weft Commands                         TRACE(1)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTracing\x1b[0m\n  The trace command aggregates lines across services in all your namespaces. A\n  bare trace lists your most recent traces. Give it a trace ID with --id and it\n  returns every log line carrying that ID in chronological order. The --spans\n  flag gives you a per-operation breakdown: of one request's duration per stage\n  with --id, or of how an operation performs across all requests without it.\n  All provided, of course, that you've annotated your log lines and set up your\n  schema to parse those annotations.\n\n    trace [<namespace>[/<service>]]                       the recent traces\n    trace --id <id> [<scope>]                             one request's log lines\n    trace --spans --id <id> [<scope>]                     where one request's time went\n    trace --spans [<scope>]                               how operations behave across requests\n    trace --spans --name <span> [--samples] [<scope>]     one operation\n\n  The optional <scope> is a namespace or a namespace/service, and narrows any\n  form: trace acme/api lists the traces that touched acme/api, trace --spans\n  acme/api aggregates only that service's spans, and trace --id <id> acme/api\n  shows only that service's lines of the request. Narrow by time with\n  range (man range): trace reads the current range, so a trace\n  older than the window needs a wider one first.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mGetting trace IDs into your logs\x1b[0m\n  If you're using one of the weft\n  SDKs (https://github.com/orgs/w3ft-net/repositories), you'll get a lot of the\n  tracing work for cheap-to-free. They will:\n\n  - adopt the traceparent header of an incoming HTTP request, or start a new\n    trace when there is none, using included middlewares\n  - provide convenience methods for conveyors those values to another service in\n    headers via HTTP or with interceptors over gRPC\n  - track span names and durations\n  - serialize all the above values onto each log line emitted by SDK-instrumented\n    methods\n\n  However, if you've already got a logging setup you're happy with and it\n  contains those values, you can just create a schema that knows how to extract\n  those values.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mFinding a trace\x1b[0m\n  A bare trace lists the last 50 traces in the current range, newest first:\n\n    $ trace\n     AGE     DUR LINES ERRORS ROOT            SERVICES                             TRACE\n     20s    1.2s     7      2 GET /orders/812 acme/api, acme/orders, acme/payments 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n      3m   840ms    12      0 POST /checkout  acme/api, acme/orders                9c1d02e87a5b4f3d8e6a1b2c3d4e5f60\n     11m     5ms     1      -                 acme/worker                          e07a4b9d1c2f43a58b6d7e8f90a1b2c3\n\n  The summary always covers the whole trace, even when you narrow the list with a\n  scope: trace acme/api picks the traces that touched acme/api, then shows each\n  one in full.\n\n  Piped, you get one JSON line per trace, so you can go hunting for slowness with jq:\n\n    {\"trace_id\":\"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\",\"start_ns\":1760882531000000000,\"end_ns\":1760882532200000000,\"dur_ns\":1200000000,\"lines\":7,\"errors\":2,\"services\":[\"acme/api\",\"acme/orders\",\"acme/payments\"],\"root\":\"GET /orders/812\"}\n\n    trace | jq -r 'select(.errors > 0) | .trace_id'\n\n  Alternatively, if you are looking at a particularly troubling log line via some\n  1-liner involving jq or AWK, you can get your trace id via\n  metavariable (man metavars).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mA single request\x1b[0m\n    $ trace --id 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n    # 7 line(s) across 3 service(s), 2026-10-19T14:02:11Z to 2026-10-19T14:02:12Z\n                     acme/api      GET /orders/812\n       +3ms          acme/orders   loading order 812\n      +24ms        █ acme/orders   db.query took 21ms\n      +80ms     ████ acme/payments charging card ending 4242\n     +160ms ████████ acme/payments charge ok\n      +40ms       ██ acme/orders   order 812 confirmed\n      +12ms          acme/api      200 OK\n\n  The first column shows time elapsed since the previous line (the first line\n  thus having no value there). On a narrow enough terminal, the time column is\n  dropped.\n\n  Like a bare trace, the form above returns JSON when piped, with $namespace and $service present as metavars:\n\n    trace --id 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 | grep -i error\n    trace --id 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736 | jq -r '$service + \": \" + .msg'\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDigging into spans for a request\x1b[0m\n    $ trace --spans --id 4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\n    NAME                                   COUNT     TOTAL ERRORS\n    api.handle (acme/api)               █      1       7ms      0\n    db.query (acme/orders)     ██████████      3      38ms      0\n    db.query (acme/payments)           ██      1       9ms      -\n    orders.load (acme/orders)          ██      1      11ms      0\n\n  In the browser, running trace --spans --id <id> will open a modal. In the\n  terminal, it opens an interactive view. Both will let you drill down on\n  specific spans. Piped, it again gives one JSON line per finished span or span\n  error:\n\n    {\"name\":\"db.query\",\"namespace\":\"acme\",\"service\":\"orders\",\"trace_id\":\"4bf9…\",\"span_id\":\"a1\",\"parent_id\":\"b2\",\"dur_ns\":21000000,\"ts_ns\":1760882531000000000,\"level\":\"INFO\"}\n\n  ts_ns is when the span ended, so it started at ts_ns - dur_ns.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mGlobal span tracking\x1b[0m\n    $ trace --spans\n    NAME                                 COUNT       AVG       P50       P99       MIN       MAX ERRORS\n    api.handle (acme/api)    ██████████   9102     1.2ms     1.1ms     4.8ms     410µs     9.9ms      0\n    db.query (acme/orders)         ████  18420     500µs     410µs     2.3ms      90µs     8.1ms      3\n\n  trace can take the --span flag without a trace id to analyze span\n  statistics across all traces in your current range. Add --name to look at\n  just one, and a scope to look at just one service:\n\n    trace --spans --name db.query\n    trace --spans acme/orders\n\n  COUNT, ERRORS, AVG, MIN and MAX are exact. P50 and P99 are\n  approximate (within 1% of the true value).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mUsing the results\x1b[0m\n  The fact that trace results yield JSON means you can filter, reshape, chart\n  or alert on it like any other source:\n\n    trace --spans --name db.query | jq '.p99_ns / 1000000' | alert above 5000 --notify=ops\n\n  Throw that in a cron (man crons) and you get paged if the 99th percentile of you\n  queries creeps past five seconds.\n"

// ManualWeftd — weftd (generated from docs/manual/weftd.md).
const ManualWeftd = "WEFTD(1)                        Weft Commands                         WEFTD(1)\n\n  weftd is weft's own log agent.  It tails files and systemd units on a Linux\n  host and streams structured rows to weft over gRPC. It is the fastest way you\n  can get your logs into weft, and since weft was designed so we could feel like\n  we were running tail -f right on the host, it's what we recommend you use if\n  you can.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConnect a host with weftd\x1b[0m\n  This page covers the weftd-specific parts; for an overview, see Connect a log\n  source (man overview).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m1. Install\x1b[0m\n  Download weftd from the Downloads page. Or just use curl:\n\n    curl -LO https://releases.w3ft.net/weftd/v0.33.0/weftd_0.33.0_amd64.deb\n    sudo dpkg -i weftd_0.33.0_amd64.deb\n\n  arm64 packages are also available -- just swap the suffix in the paths above.\n\n  The package installs the systemd unit and enables it on boot, but does not\n  start it: weftd won't run until it's configured.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m2. Create a token\x1b[0m\n  Create a namespaced token as described in Connect a log\n  source (man namespaces). Put token: in\n  /etc/weftd/config.yaml, or supply it as WEFTD_TOKEN (for example in\n  /etc/default/weftd). The environment value wins, so a secret injector can\n  provide the token without templating the YAML.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208m3. Configure and start\x1b[0m\n  weftd reads /etc/weftd/config.yaml. A minimal config needs only a token and\n  at least one source:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    token: wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\n\n    sources:\n      - service: myapp\n        path: /var/log/myapp/app.log\n\n  Then start it with sudo systemctl start weftd. Rows arrive within a few\n  seconds. weftd requires a schema, so approve\n  one (man overview) once the source has\n  sampled.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding your logs\x1b[0m\n  Weft maps weftd sources to log files as follows:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mweftd source                            weft file\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  path: /var/log/myapp/api.log            /log/<ns>/api/main.log\n  path: /var/log/myapp/api.log, service:  /log/<ns>/myapp/main.log\n  myapp\n  path: /var/log/myapp/audit.log,         /log/<ns>/myapp/audit.log\n  service: myapp, kind: audit\n  unit: myapp-worker.service              /log/<ns>/myapp-worker/main.log\n  path: /var/log/myapp/*.log              /log/<ns>/api/main.log,\n                                          /log/<ns>/worker/main.log, one per\n                                          file\n  path: /var/log/*/access.log             /log/<ns>/api/access/main.log,\n                                          /log/<ns>/web/access/main.log\n  deployment: web with a metrics: block   /log/<ns>/web/host.log, disk.log and\n                                          network.log\n\n  Every row carries instance, the host's name, so a single\n  tail /log/<ns>/myapp/main.log covers every host.\n\n  service: is ignored on a glob; the service is derived per file from its path\n  below the pattern's fixed directory, minus the extension. To make several\n  files share one service, list them as separate non-glob sources with the same\n  service:.\n\n  (The presence of the <env> segment depends on how you created your token.\n  See Paths (man introduction) for the layout rules.)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mConfiguration\x1b[0m\n  General, box-level settings are top-level keys:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey            Definition                      Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------  ------------------------------  -------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  token          Ingest token for your           none; required\n                 namespace. May instead come\n                 from the WEFTD_TOKEN\n                 environment variable (see\n                 Connect a host).\n  auto_update    Self-update from                true\n                 releases.w3ft.net (see\n                 Updates). false pins this host\n                 to the installed version.\n  deployment     The fleet or scaling unit this  empty\n                 agent belongs to (k8s\n                 Deployment, AWS ASG, etc.).\n                 Stamped on every row, and the\n                 service host metrics are\n                 written under.\n  region         Region stamped on every row.    auto-detected from cloud\n                                                 metadata at startup\n  socket         Path of the records socket for  none; socket disabled\n                 your apps (see App API).\n  metrics        Host metrics: cpu, memory,      no block: off\n                 disk, load, network. Having\n                 the block turns them on (see\n                 System metrics).\n  metavariables  Values for the deployment,      none\n                 version, region, cluster and\n                 node columns, each read from\n                 the environment, a file or a\n                 JSON file (see Metavariables).\n                 Example: version: {file:\n                 /opt/myapp/VERSION}\n  checkpoint     Tuning for                      see that section\n                 resume-after-restart, which is\n                 always on (see Resuming after\n                 a restart).\n  sources        The list of things to tail.     none; required\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSources\x1b[0m\n  A host can have any number of sources, listed under the sources key. Each\n  entry tails one thing. Set exactly one of path or unit.\n\n    sources:\n      - service: myapp\n        path: /var/log/myapp/app.log\n        version:\n          file: /opt/myapp/VERSION\n\n      - service: nginx\n        path: /var/log/nginx/access.log\n\n      - unit: myapp-worker.service\n        kind: main\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey            Definition                      Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------------  ------------------------------  -------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  path           A file or glob to tail.         none\n                 Mutually exclusive with unit.\n  unit           A systemd unit to read from     none\n                 the journal. Mutually\n                 exclusive with path.\n  service        The <service> segment of the    file name without extension, or\n                 source's file path in weft      unit name without .service\n                 (see Finding your logs).\n                 Ignored on a glob.\n  kind           The file part of the            main\n                 pseudofile,\n                 /log/<ns>/<service>/<kind>.log.\n                 heartbeats, runtime, host,\n                 disk and network are reserved\n                 (the last three hold host\n                 metrics).\n  metavariables  This source's own values for    none\n                 deployment, version, region,\n                 cluster and node, overriding\n                 the host-wide ones (see\n                 Metavariables).\n\n  Every source, whatever its kind, handles lines the same way. CRLF endings are\n  removed, blank lines are skipped, and leading and trailing whitespace is\n  trimmed from every line, so indentation (a stack trace's leading spaces, say)\n  does not survive. A line longer than 1 MiB is cut at 1 MiB and ends with\n  …[line truncated].\n\n  Some behaviors vary by source type:\n\n  - For concrete files (path: /var/log/myapp/app.log), the tailer keeps exact byte\n    offsets, follows logrotate in both styles (rename-and-recreate and\n    copytruncate), and drains the old file before it switches to the new one.\n    A line is shipped only once its newline has been written.\n  - For glob patterns (path: /var/log/myapp/*.log), *, ? and [...] work as in\n    the shell, and ** matches any number of directories. Files that exist at\n    startup are tailed from the saved position. A file created later in the\n    directory the pattern starts from is picked up automatically and read from\n    its first line. A file created in a deeper directory after startup is\n    found at the next restart. If a concrete file is also named by its own\n    non-glob source, that explicit source wins and the glob skips it. Each\n    matched file behaves as a single file does.\n  - For systemd unit (unit: myapp.service), weftd runs\n    journalctl -f -u <unit> and ships new journal lines from the moment it\n    starts. Unit sources are not checkpointed (see\n    Delivery below), so a restart does not replay the journal, and\n    journalctl must be on the host.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mMetavariables\x1b[0m\n  Weft refers to dimensions as metavariables (man metavars) because of the\n  first-class support they have in our awk and jq implementations. Using weftd\n  enables you to set more of them than via other producers. The configuration for\n  this lives in weftd.yaml.\n\n  region comes from a top-level config key when set. When not set, weftd asks\n  the cloud metadata service once at startup when running on AWS, GCP, or Azure. Another set of metavariables (deployment, version, region, cluster and\n  node) can be set for the whole host, or for a single source, via one of three\n  mechanisms:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey   Behavior                             Refreshed\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----  -----------------------------------  -----------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  env   weftd reads the supplied             Once, at startup. Changing it needs\n        environment variable                 a weftd restart.\n  file  weftd reads the contents of the      Whenever the file changes.\n        file and trims whitespace\n  json  weftd reads JSON from a file and     Whenever the file changes.\n        extract a value at the supplied\n        json path\n\n  Examples:\n\n    metavariables:\n      version:\n        file: /opt/myapp/VERSION          # your deploy writes e.g. \"2026.10.18-3\"\n      node:\n        json: /opt/app/data/shard.json:.shard_id\n      cluster:\n        env: CLUSTER_NAME\n\n  A json: path is a minimal subset of jq: field access (.foo), nested\n  fields (.foo.bar) and array indices (.items[0].name). For anything\n  richer, pre-process the file with your own tooling and point weftd at the\n  result.\n\n  If you have metavariables defined at both the host level and for individual\n  sources, the source values will win. Rows that belong to no source, such as\n  host metrics, only ever get the host-wide values.\n\n    metavariables:\n      cluster:\n        env: CLUSTER_NAME               # every source on this host\n\n    sources:\n      - service: api\n        path: /var/log/api/*.log\n        metavariables:\n          version:\n            file: /opt/api/VERSION      # just api\n      - service: worker\n        path: /var/log/worker.log\n        metavariables:\n          version:\n            file: /opt/worker/VERSION   # just worker\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mOther features\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSystem metrics (optional)\x1b[0m\n  weftd can also ship host metrics alongside log lines. Add a top-level\n  metrics: block to turn them on, and delete it to turn them off. An empty\n  block (metrics: {}) runs everything with the defaults:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    token: wft_a1b2c3d4e5f6...\n    deployment: web\n\n    metrics:\n      interval: 30s\n\n    sources:\n      - service: api\n        path: /var/log/api/*.log\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mKey         Definition                                                Default\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----------  --------------------------------------------------------  --------\x1b[0m\n  interval    How often to sample, as a duration. A value that doesn't  30s\n              parse falls back to the default.\n  collectors  Which of cpu, memory, disk, load, network to run.         all five\n              Unknown names are ignored.\n\n  Samples are read from /proc (Linux only) and streamed as JSON log rows.\n  Metrics go under your deployment, as if it were a service.\n  A host with deployment: web writes\n  /log/<namespace>/web/{host,disk,network}.log. deployment has to be set for\n  metrics to run; without one weftd logs an error and writes nothing. Depending\n  on which collectors are enabled, your metrics will end up in:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mFile         One row per                      Contents\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-----------  -------------------------------  --------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  host.log     tick                             cpu, memory and load objects\n  disk.log     /dev/* mount, per tick           path, total_gb, used_gb, pct\n  network.log  non-loopback interface, per      iface, rx_bytes_sec,\n               tick                             tx_bytes_sec, rx_bytes_total,\n                                                tx_bytes_total\n\n  The objects in host.log:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mObject  Fields\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------  ----------------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  cpu     user, system, idle, iowait (percent), cores, time_s (cumulative busy\n          CPU seconds)\n  memory  total_mb, used_mb, available_mb, swap_used_mb, pct\n  load    load1, load5, load15\n\n  host.log holds cpu, memory and load as nested objects, so the first\n  tick has no cpu (it needs two samples to compute a delta) and a collector you\n  turned off has no object. network.log needs two samples too, so it's empty\n  for the first tick after startup.\n\n    # Recent memory pressure\n    tail /log/myns/web/host.log | jq '.memory.pct'\n\n    # Per-host, per-mount disk usage, alerting above 80%\n    tail /log/myns/web/disk.log \\\n      | jq -r '\"\\($host):\\(.path) \\(.pct)\"' \\\n      | alert above 80 -v 2 -t 1 --notify=ops\n\n    # Recent load averages by host\n    tail -n 1000 /log/myns/web/host.log | jq '{host: $host, load5: .load.load5}'\n\n    # One host's disks\n    tail /log/myns/web/disk.log | jq 'select($host == \"i-0abc\")'\n\n  Metrics only run on Linux (the collectors read /proc/stat,\n  /proc/meminfo, /proc/mounts, /proc/loadavg, and\n  /proc/net/dev). On other kernels the block is ignored.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSocket listener\x1b[0m\n  weftd can listen on a local Unix socket so your own applications can use the\n  weft SDKs (https://github.com/orgs/w3ft-net/repositories) to annotate traces,\n  spans, and timings and send events, heartbeats and runtime stats over the same\n  streaming connection weftd already has open.  Name the socket in config.yaml\n  and weftd will create it on startup:\n\n    # /etc/weftd/config.yaml\n    socket: /var/run/weftd.sock\n\n  Some socket properties:\n\n  - It's world-writable\n  - It's one-way -- weft never sends anything back\n  - It buffers up to 4096 lines, after which, if undrained, writes will silently fail\n\n  Though most of the convenience comes from using the weft SDKs, the enterprising\n  user can always write directly to this socket themselves. Connect to the socket\n  and write one line per message, each ending in a newline:\n\n    <app>:<line>\\n\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mPart  Meaning\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m----  ------------------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  app   service or service/kind, where kind defaults to main. It must be shorter\n        than 32 characters and contain no spaces. A line with no valid app:\n        prefix goes to the default app.\n  line  The log line, with no newline in it. A line over 1 MiB closes the\n        connection.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mLine header\x1b[0m\n  The weft SDKs write and weftd reads a per-line header carrying metadata for\n  that line. It writes those headers when it writes to log files and when it\n  writes to the socket.\n\n    \\x1e v1 \\x1f ts \\x1f level \\x1f trace \\x1f span_name \\x1f span_id \\x1f parent_id \\x1f dur_ns \\x1f fmt \\x1f kind \\x1e body\n\n  The header sits between two \\x1e bytes, with its values separated by \\x1f,\n  and the spaces above are only for reading. On the socket the header comes right\n  after <app>:.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mValue      Meaning\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m---------  -------------------------------------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  v1         The version. It fixes the order and meaning of the values that\n             follow.\n  ts         When the line happened, as integer Unix nanoseconds. Empty means\n             weftd stamps it on arrival.\n  level      The severity as the writer spells it, such as INFO, WARN or ERROR.\n  trace      The trace ID the line belongs to.\n  span_name  The name of the span this line describes, for a line that starts or\n             ends a span.\n  span_id    The span's ID.\n  parent_id  The ID of the span's parent.\n  dur_ns     The span's duration in nanoseconds, on the line that ends it.\n  fmt        json or text, the shape of the body.\n  kind       A second stream for the same service, such as queries for\n             service/queries. It uses letters, digits, - and _ (up to 64\n             characters) and can't be heartbeats, runtime, host, disk or\n             network.\n\n  Every value after v1 is optional. A value that doesn't parse, such as a ts\n  that isn't a number or a dur_ns that isn't positive, is discarded without\n  rejecting the line itself. Header field values can't contain \\x1e, \\x1f or\n  a newline.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mBehavior and semantics\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mDelivery\x1b[0m\n  Files are the only source with delivery guarantees. weftd keeps recently sent\n  rows (up to 8 MiB per file) until the coordinator acknowledges them, at the\n  commit_interval below, and only then advances the saved position. Anything\n  unacknowledged is resent on a new stream, so delivery is at-least-once.  In the\n  event of an outage, weftd stops reading when its window is full and resumes\n  when the upstream is back. Unit (journalctl), records-socket and metrics rows\n  are best-effort: they are sent straight through and are not replayed after a\n  restart or an outage, whereas log files on disk are checkpointed (see below).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mResuming after a restart\x1b[0m\n  weftd remembers how far it has delivered each tailed file, so a restart picks\n  up where it left off and reads whatever was written in the meantime. The saved\n  position means \"weft acknowledged everything up to here\", so lines\n  that were in flight when weftd stopped are sent again.\n\n  State is kept in /var/lib/weftd/checkpoints/, which is created by the debian\n  package (StateDirectory=weftd). Checkpointing is always on. These settings,\n  all optional, tune it:\n\n    checkpoint:\n      dir: /var/lib/weftd/checkpoints   # where state.json lives\n      flush_interval: 5s                # how often positions are written to disk\n      commit_interval: 15s              # how often delivered lines are acknowledged\n      max_resume_bytes: 256MiB          # per-file cap on backlog replayed after downtime\n\n  What weftd does for each file when it starts:\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mSituation                               Starts at\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------------------  --------------------------------------\x1b[0m\n  First start with checkpointing (no      the end of the file\n  state yet)\n  File has no saved position (appeared    the beginning\n  while weftd was down)\n  File matches its saved position         the saved position\n  File is shorter than the saved          the beginning\n  position (truncated)\n  File was replaced while weftd was down  the beginning; the old file's unread\n  (rotated)                               tail is not recovered\n  More than max_resume_bytes behind       the newest max_resume_bytes, from a\n                                          line boundary\n\n  A file is matched by device, inode and a hash of its first kilobyte, so a\n  reused inode is not mistaken for the same file. Files created while weftd is\n  running are read from their first line. Every skip or replay is logged to\n  weftd's own log (journalctl -u weftd), and summarized once a minute as a\n  checkpoint stats line per file.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mUpdates\x1b[0m\n  Once a day (or on restart) weftd checks releases.w3ft.net for a newer\n  release. By default it downloads the build, verifies its SHA-256, replaces the\n  running binary, and exits so systemd (Restart=always) relaunches it. Set\n  auto_update: false to pin a host.  It then only logs a line when a newer\n  version exists.\n"

